work', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/289b5e54-0683-5dc3-bdad-7cdaa9e8de2d', 'word_count': 429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/289b5e54-0683-5dc3-bdad-7cdaa9e8de2d'}, {'abstract': 'For many of us, it’s synonymous with war and strife. But for the artists, chefs, designers, architects and scholars who live there, Beirut will always be a place where ideas and beauty flourished ... and flourish still.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/02/t-magazine/travel-beirut-architecture-art-design.html', 'snippet': 'For many of us, it’s synonymous with war and strife. But for the artists, chefs, designers, architects and scholars who live there, Beirut will always be a place where ideas and beauty flourished ... and flourish still.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE DAY I ARRIVED in Beirut I was collected at my hotel by Huda Baroudi, a cheerful woman who had offered to show me around. It was a lazy Sunday, grim and gray, and I was jet-lagged. But her eyes were shining and she was eager to take me to the Bechara el-Khoury Mansion, a 19th-\xadcentury villa that long ago — before it had been abandoned, pillaged and finally shelled during the civil war — was one of Beirut’s grand residences.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '144', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/02/t-magazine/beirut-slide-QQZP/beirut-slide-QQZP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 434, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/02/t-magazine/beirut-slide-QQZP/beirut-slide-QQZP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 434}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/02/t-magazine/beirut-slide-QQZP/beirut-slide-QQZP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/02/t-magazine/beirut-slide-QQZP/beirut-slide-QQZP-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1481, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/02/t-magazine/beirut-slide-QQZP/beirut-slide-QQZP-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/02/t-magazine/beirut-slide-QQZP/beirut-slide-QQZP-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/02/t-magazine/beirut-slide-QQZP/beirut-slide-QQZP-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Eternal Magic of Beirut', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tale of Two Cities', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beirut (Lebanon)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hariri, Rafik', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lebanon', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-02T09:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Specter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Specter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a70a08e6-326d-5b0a-b665-c9cd955fd8b7', 'word_count': 4129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a70a08e6-326d-5b0a-b665-c9cd955fd8b7'}, {'abstract': 'Sometimes the baby you have is not what you expected when you were expecting.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/05/02/when-parents-blame-themselves-for-that-difficult-baby/', 'snippet': 'Sometimes the baby you have is not what you expected when you were expecting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In March, I wrote in this column about how different babies are different assignments, some much more challenging than others. Does a difficult baby mean parents are doing something wrong? How do you know when a baby — or small child’s — behavior signals problems more serious than a challenging temperament? And when nothing major is actually “wrong,” do parents have any real reason to complain?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/04/28/health/wellbaby/wellbaby-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/04/28/health/wellbaby/wellbaby-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/04/28/health/wellbaby/wellbaby-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/04/28/health/wellbaby/wellbaby-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/04/28/health/wellbaby/wellbaby-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/04/28/health/wellbaby/wellbaby-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/04/28/health/wellbaby/wellbaby-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Parents Blame Themselves for That ‘Difficult’ Baby', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-02T09:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Perri Klass, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Perri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klass', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed2618f6-ad9d-52e3-b32e-722f7037fa20', 'word_count': 1076, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed2618f6-ad9d-52e3-b32e-722f7037fa20'}, {'abstract': 'Throughout the country, communities are being retrofitted to accommodate the tsunami of elders expected to live there as baby boomers age.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/05/02/aging-in-place/', 'snippet': 'Throughout the country, communities are being retrofitted to accommodate the tsunami of elders expected to live there as baby boomers age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I asked the other three members of my walking group, all of whom are in their mid to upper 70s, whether they had any concerns about future living arrangements, they each said they had none despite the fact that, like me, they live in multistory private homes without elevators and, in two cases, without bathrooms on every floor.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page'