e, rather than storing it underground, to fight climate change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERKELEY, Calif. — Think, for a moment, of carbon dioxide as garbage, a waste product from burning fossil fuels. Like other garbage, almost all of that CO2 is thrown away — into the atmosphere, where it contributes to climate change. A small amount is captured and stored underground to keep it out of the air.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/science/03FUEL/03FUEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/03/science/03FUEL/03FUEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 436}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/science/03FUEL/03FUEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 745, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/science/03FUEL/03FUEL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1490, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/science/03FUEL/03FUEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/03/science/03FUEL/03FUEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/science/03FUEL/03FUEL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Researchers Aim to Put Carbon Dioxide Back to Work', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Recycling Nature’s Garbage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'X Prize Foundation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-02T19:38:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry Fountain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fountain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/232f8391-3f8c-55e8-b7de-de834326b7ec', 'word_count': 1395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/232f8391-3f8c-55e8-b7de-de834326b7ec'}, {'abstract': 'Al Held’s elegant, calligraphic brush-stroke drawings — a contrast to his signature “concrete” style — are on view next week at Van Doren Waxter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/02/t-magazine/art/al-held-brushstroke-drawings.html', 'snippet': 'Al Held’s elegant, calligraphic brush-stroke drawings — a contrast to his signature “concrete” style — are on view next week at Van Doren Waxter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By 1960, Al Held had the New York art scene sitting upright. His “Taxi Cab” series, riots of impasto geometry, were an unequivocal “feh” to the fashionable ideas of the New York School and the first inkling of what would become his signature hard-lined, “concrete” style — something that caught 