'snippet': 'In the West, racists see skin color. In Algeria, they see religion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ORAN, Algeria — For a few years now, families of migrants from sub-Saharan Africa have been gathering at major street crossings in the large cities of northern Algeria. They come to beg for alms, wearing grotesque outfits: oversize veils for the women, even little girls; cotton djellabas for the men; prayer beads ostentatiously displayed. They say “Allah” too readily and misquote verses from the Koran.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Black in Algeria? Then You’d Better Be Muslim', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Being Black in the Land of Allah', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daoud, Kamel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALGERIA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-02T20:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kamel Daoud', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kamel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daoud', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a357af9c-4d3e-5671-8d58-481b4cca61e8', 'word_count': 1034, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a357af9c-4d3e-5671-8d58-481b4cca61e8'}, {'abstract': 'En occident, les racistes voient la peau. En Algérie, ils voient la religion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/03/opinion/kamel-daoud-noir-en-algerie-mieux-vaut-etre-musulman.html', 'snippet': 'En occident, les racistes voient la peau. En Algérie, ils voient la religion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ORAN, Algérie — Depuis quelques années, on voit aux croisements des rues des grandes villes du nord de l’Algérie des familles de migrants originaires du sud du Sahara. Ils viennent mendier, vêtus d’accoutrements grotesques : voiles démesurés pour les femmes, même les fillettes ; djellabas en tissu pour les hommes ; chapelets affichés de manière ostentatoire. Ils ont le « Allah » trop facile et se trompent en récitant des versets du Coran.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/opinion/03daoud/03daoud-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kamel Daoud: Noir en Algérie? Mieux vaut être musulman.', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, '