y', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Reed and Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85d157a2-eed3-521a-a893-15cfa6ee99e7', 'word_count': 1306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85d157a2-eed3-521a-a893-15cfa6ee99e7'}, {'abstract': 'The attack on a maternity hospital, which killed at least three women, was the sixth assault on a medical facility in the divided Syrian city in less than a week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/04/world/middleeast/maternity-hospital-attack-aleppo-syria.html', 'snippet': 'The attack on a maternity hospital, which killed at least three women, was the sixth assault on a medical facility in the divided Syrian city in less than a week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — Insurgent shelling hit a maternity hospital in the government-held section of the Syrian city of Aleppo on Tuesday, according to state media and footage from the scene, underscoring what rights groups are calling a growing disregard for the rules of war.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Aleppo Hospital Is Hit, This Time on Government-Held Side', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Another Hospital Is Hit in Latest Aleppo Violence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALEPPO (SYRIA)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-03T19:31:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Barnard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d663eb47-a02f-5263-98c5-387f8d06bb48', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d663eb47-a02f-5263-98c5-387f8d06bb48'}, {'abstract': 'Through Climate Feedback, climate scientists are trying to build a layer of veracity over online news and commentary — but readers have to want the truth.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/05/03/scientists-build-a-hype-detector-for-online-climate-news-and-commentary/', 'snippet': 'Through Climate Feedback, climate scientists are trying to build a layer of veracity over online news and commentary — but readers have to want the truth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As longtime readers know, a prime focus on Dot Earth has been testing ways to clarify disputes over consequential science — a need that’s amplified in complex arenas laden by persistent uncertainty. It’s not quite a law of physics yet, but a there’s a consistent pattern in such cases in which a paucity of evidence leads to an overabundance of assertion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/blogs/dottracker/dottracker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 753, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/03/blogs/dottracker/dottracker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 753}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/blogs/dottracker/dottracker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 6