 nominees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04TONYSLUPITA/04TONYSLUPITA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04TONYSLUPITA/04TONYSLUPITA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04TONYSLUPITA/04TONYSLUPITA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04TONYSLUPITA/04TONYSLUPITA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04TONYSLUPITA/04TONYSLUPITA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04TONYSLUPITA/04TONYSLUPITA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04TONYSLUPITA/04TONYSLUPITA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘I Guess It Really Did Happen’: Tony Award Nominees React', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-03T19:47:22+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Theater', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By ERIK PIEPENBURG and MICHAEL PAULSON', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'PIEPENBURG', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'PAULSON', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/a74f0c86-a76a-59da-a226-1cc86a966378', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/a74f0c86-a76a-59da-a226-1cc86a966378'}, {'abstract': 'The conservator Gloria Velandia works with Frieze, Art Basel and the Armory to ensure works are in top condition — and million-dollar deals go through.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/03/t-magazine/art/frieze-art-basel-armory-art-fairs-conservator-gloria-velandia.html', 'snippet': 'The conservator Gloria Velandia works with Frieze, Art Basel and the Armory to ensure works are in top condition — and million-dollar deals go through.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At an art fair, there are many visible roles in play: director, exhibitor, client, critic, installer. But an undersung — and increasingly critical — part is the one inhabited by the conservator Gloria Velandia and her team of trained assistants. Almost invisible, except to those in the know, Velandia runs the international circuit as the preferred conservator for major-league fairs (including Frieze New York, which begins this Thursday). “I’m the doctor on call,” Velandia says of her unique position under the fair tent. “My role is primarily to help make sure, one, people have an understanding of what they are purchasing, that would be for condition reporting; and two, helping ensure that the artworks are in the best condition for display and sale.” Her now-ubiquitous presence at fairs came out of a casual conversation in Paris with the former Art Basel director Sam Keller. “Who does something for the exhibitors if something happens?” she asked Keller, referring to works being damaged, however slightly, during the bustle of the fair. “He said they had no one, and invited me to give it a try.” Sixteen years later, Velandia is the go-to for Art Basel, Frieze and the Armory — with a master’s degree in chemistry and conservation, she’s the person you want on the scene when a vacuum-cleaner hose fondles a floor piece or a painting gets hassled during the V.I.P. bum rush.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-conservator-01/03tmag-conservator-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-conservator-01/03tmag-conservator-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-conservator-01/03tmag-conservator-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-conservator-01/03tmag-conservator-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-conservator-01/03tmag-conservator-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-conservator-01/03tmag-conservator-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 