move around a lot,” Lisa Spellman says with a laugh. The beloved gallerist and her 32-year-old institution, 303 Gallery, were among the first art spaces to adopt Chelsea as a new home (from SoHo, of course, in 1996), and remained in the same space on 22nd Street for 15 years. But the last three have been a game of real estate musical chairs. “It’s so boring, but everyone always wants to talk about real estate,” quips Spellman, who in addition to launching the careers of heavyweight artists Christopher Wool, Robert Gober, Doug Aitken and Karen Kilimnik, has played a maverick hand in the continuous poker game known as the New York real estate market. “It’s being a New Yorker for so long — it’s a second language!”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-gallery01/03tmag-gallery01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-gallery01/03tmag-gallery01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-gallery01/03tmag-gallery01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-gallery01/03tmag-gallery01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/03/t-magazine/03tmag-gallery01/03tmag-gallery01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/03/t-mag