995', 'word_count': 1920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/248659f6-cc72-5a9b-bad3-3e47c65fe995'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Russo’s delightful sequel to “Nobody’s Fool” features the further adventures of Sully and other familiar residents of North Bath, N.Y.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/05/books/review-richard-russo-returns-upstate-in-everybodys-fool.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Russo’s delightful sequel to “Nobody’s Fool” features the further adventures of Sully and other familiar residents of North Bath, N.Y.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the years since he dependably turned out small-town American classics like “Nobody’s Fool” (1993) and the Pulitzer Prize-winning “Empire Falls” (2001), Richard Russo has spent a lot of time wandering in the wilderness. He has written only one novel, “Bridge of Sighs” (2007), with anything like the scope, hilarity, anguished underpinnings and brilliantly adroit nonchalance of his early work. He has written enough screen adaptations to let Hollywood-style structure seep into his fiction. And he has mostly avoided the rambling, winsome stories and foxy stumblebums who made readers fall in love with his books in the first place.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/08/books/08russo-sub/08russo-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/08/books/08russo-sub/08russo-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/08/books/08russo-sub/08russo-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/08/books/08russo-sub/08russo-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1571, 'width': 1047, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/08/books/08russo-sub/08russo-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/08/books/08russo-sub/08russo-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/08/books/08russo-sub/08russo-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Richard Russo Returns Upstate in ‘Everybody’s Fool’', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Back Upstate, a Town and Its Residents Continue to Crumble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Russo, Richard (1949- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Everybody's Fool (Book)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-04T21:21:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/67d79d4a-60d7-502e-91d5-09293ee6d1fe', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67d79d4a-60d7-502e-91d5-09293ee6d1fe'}, {'abstract': 'This new comic drama on Seeso explores a family that makes the characters on “Modern Family” seem downright normal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/05/arts/television/review-flowers-features-a-household-in-the-twilight-zone.html', 'snippet': 'This new comic drama on Seeso explores a family that makes the characters on “Modern Family” seem downright normal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The history of television sometimes seems as if it were one long experiment in how dysfunctional a fictional family can be and still constitute a coherent TV show. At first, the landscape had mostly plain-vanilla families — in every sense — with the occasional widower thrown in. Then came “The Munsters” and “The Addams Family,” forebears, in a way, of more outlandish and gritty families, like the Gallaghers of Showtime’s “Shameless.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/05/arts/05FLOWERS2/05FLOWERS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/05/arts/05FLOWERS2/05FLOWERS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/05/arts/05FLOWERS2/05FLOWERS2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/05/arts/05FLOWERS2/05FLOWERS2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1298, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/05/arts/05FLOWERS2/05FLOWERS2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/05/arts/05FLOWERS2/05FLOWERS2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLar