document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ali Benjamin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ali', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benjamin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3b070dc-adaf-5051-bdd0-0498ad5c8710', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3b070dc-adaf-5051-bdd0-0498ad5c8710'}, {'abstract': 'Four books offer different takes on the challenging subjects of death and mourning.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/08/books/review/cry-heart-but-never-break-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Four books offer different takes on the challenging subjects of death and mourning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You don’t want to talk to your kids about death. Not your death (dreary thought), their death (unspeakable), death in general: It’s all bad. They, however, may be undaunted, even unnervingly fascinated, by the bottomless mystery of inert forever-ness. (They are, after all, children.) Luckily, without any help from you, today’s tots will soon enough stray onto a vast field of inquiry — literature — fervently devoted to the insult of mortality. In the meantime, you’ll find that while picture books have grown increasingly frank on sensitive topics of family structure, disability, race and ethnicity, death remains largely absent, replicating your own woeful silence.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/08/books/review/0508-BKS-Levine01/0508-BKS-Levine01-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/08/books/review/0508-BKS-Levine01/0508-BKS-Levine01-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight':