ions', 'value': 'Etsy Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Fred (1954- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Catskill (NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-05T15:19:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Penelope Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Penelope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba1beea3-fe40-5f4d-a6af-85d65d41bb4a', 'word_count': 2623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba1beea3-fe40-5f4d-a6af-85d65d41bb4a'}, {'abstract': 'How does fiction handle terrorism? The Lebanese novelist Rabee Jaber points to the mundane nature of brutality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/08/books/review/remade-by-terror.html', 'snippet': 'How does fiction handle terrorism? The Lebanese novelist Rabee Jaber points to the mundane nature of brutality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The woman in the photograph doesn’t look frightened, even though she has reason to be; on the other side of the crumbling wall behind her, a small boy is pointing a big gun at someone just outside the frame. Something in her posture, however, the way she cranes her head to look at the boy, suggests impatience more than anything else. She’s on her way home from shopping, it seems, and carrying a large purse and a pale, heavy object — a \xadcauliflower? She’s standing on rubble, but her clean white sandals and beautiful bare legs gleam.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '43', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/08/books/review/0508-BKS-RovingEye/0508-BKS-RovingEye-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge':