9-c30e6b5542d6', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9ad0c36-0627-5187-b6c9-c30e6b5542d6'}, {'abstract': 'Just when you think the Lyon family can’t get more dramatic, enter someone new.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/05/arts/television/empire-season-2-episode-16-recap.html', 'snippet': 'Just when you think the Lyon family can’t get more dramatic, enter someone new.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is truth? The idea of what it is and isn’t frames “The Lyon Who Cried Wolf” in several ways. As a result, the viewer is left with more questions than answers. Jamal proclaims, “I work damn hard to walk in my truth” as an openly gay man. Yet, he enters into a “situationship” (not an official relationship but maybe more than friends with benefits) with D Major, a man who prefers to keep his sexuality a secret. Considering his character’s arc this season, the choice of his new love interest feels like a step backward. Then again, at some point, most people compromise some of their truths in the pursuit of love and companionship. So maybe there’s room for both of their truths to coexist?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04empire/04empire-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04empire/04empire-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 415}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04empire/04empire-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/04/arts/04empire/04empire-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1418, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank'