 instructions not to admit mistakes and a proposed rule to stop all coverage related to the torture and murder of an Italian student.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt’s Guidelines for Repression', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt’s Guidelines for Repression', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sisi, Abdel Fattah el-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-05T16:29:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4701ffa5-afb4-5ca2-b694-c9adb03a0ce8', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4701ffa5-afb4-5ca2-b694-c9adb03a0ce8'}, {'abstract': 'A former mayor of Rome says the investigation of housing files was begun in his administration.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/06/opinion/romes-public-housing.html', 'snippet': 'A former mayor of Rome says the investigation of housing files was begun in his administration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rome’s Public Housing', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rome’s Public Housing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public and Subsidized Housing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-05T16:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/13db7fc4-a043-5e17-903a-ceea83990545', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13db7fc4-a043-5e17-903a-ceea83990545'}, {'abstract': 'T highlights six first-time vendors to look out for this weekend at Mack Sennet Studios.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/05/t-magazine/echo-park-craft-fair-highlights.html', 'snippet': 'T highlights six first-time vendors to look out for this weekend at Mack Sennet Studios.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Los Angelenos have come to see the Echo Park Craft Fair as a kind of pilgrimage: a semiannual bonanza for independent goods from makers, crafters, designers and chefs. This Saturday and Sunday, crowds will head to Mack Sennet Studios for the market — which was founded in 2009 by Beatrice Valenzuela and Rachel Craven — for what promises be the fair’s biggest, and most varied, selection yet. “I wanted to become an exhibitor because I believe in the good that Rachel and Beatrice have created and continue to create with each fair, and with the personal work that they do,” says Lauri Kranz of Edible Gardens LA, a new vendor who will be participating this year. “It is very special, this passion that they have and then share with the rest of us.” The Kosas Cosmetics founder Sheena Yaitanes agrees: “I jumped at the chance to become an exhibitor,” she says. “It will be a joy to be in the presence of talented artists.” Here, a look at six first-time vendors that won’t get lost in the mix.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/06/t-magazine/06tmag-echopark-slide-CIVP/06tmag-echopark-slide-CIVP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/06/t-magazine/06tmag-echopark-slide-CIVP/06tmag-echopark-slide-CIVP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None