'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5fa6447-0873-5feb-a009-f83a06f483ec', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5fa6447-0873-5feb-a009-f83a06f483ec'}, {'abstract': 'Why the identities of the Marines in Joe Rosenthal’s iconic photo at Iwo Jima matter to vets like me.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/06/opinion/the-stories-we-carry.html', 'snippet': 'Why the identities of the Marines in Joe Rosenthal’s iconic photo at Iwo Jima matter to vets like me.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 2010, the troop surge in Afghanistan was underway, and for months my infantry unit — part of the 101st Airborne Division — had been training for what we were told was going to be a big and possibly bloody fight. On a tiny base in Kyrgyzstan, the day before we flew into Afghanistan, I met a soldier who had just left the district in Kandahar where my unit was headed for a yearlong tour. He told me it was bad, and I could tell that he wasn’t lying. He said a lot of us were going to die.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/06/opinion/06linehanWeb/06linehanWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 461, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/06/opinion/06linehanWeb/06linehanWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 461}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/06/opinion/06linehanWeb/06linehanWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 787, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 