n', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Naked Is Best for Fava Beans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanis, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fava Beans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-06T15:24:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Tanis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/250b7c16-469d-5c35-83b2-6215fefb4513', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/250b7c16-469d-5c35-83b2-6215fefb4513'}, {'abstract': 'The Dutch primatologist Frans de Waal, whose new book is No. 15 on the hardcover nonfiction list, says: “We have this long tradition of downplaying what animals do.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/15/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'The Dutch primatologist Frans de Waal, whose new book is No. 15 on the hardcover nonfiction list, says: “We have this long tradition of downplaying what animals do.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Human Animal: The Dutch primatologist Frans de Waal hits the hardcover nonfiction list with his survey of animal cognition, “Are We Smart Enough to Know How Smart Animals Are?,” new at No.\u202f15. It’s his first best seller, after a shelf’s worth of books devoted mainly to primates (including humans). Why expand to other species? As \xad\xad\xadde\u202fWaal told the website of the quarterly magazine Earth Island Journal, he wanted to show just how far science has come in the past 25 years. “There was a time when we were not allowed to talk about higher intelligence in animals,” he said. “Until the 1980s, I would say, we weren’t even allowed to use the term ‘animal cognition.’ Human cognition, yes, human cognition was good, but animals didn’t have cognition. Animals either had instinct or learning, and that’s all they had. .\u2008.\u2008. We have this long tradition of downplaying what animals do and considering animals dumb and below our level, and we consider ourselves as absolutely unique.” But now, he said, “everything has changed.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/books/review/15insidelist-photo/15insidelist-photo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 261, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/books/review/15insidelist-photo/15insidelist-photo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 261, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/books/review/15insidelist-photo/15insidelist-photo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/15/books/review/15insidelist-photo/15insidelist-photo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/books/review/15insidelist-photo/15insidelist-photo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Waal, F