 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'North', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2868c32f-aa74-56b0-9c25-eaecd0070972', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2868c32f-aa74-56b0-9c25-eaecd0070972'}, {'abstract': 'The plot of the Energy Transfer-Williams Companies merger unfolds like an episode of “Seinfeld.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/07/business/dealbook/the-rise-of-the-costanza-defense.html', 'snippet': 'The plot of the Energy Transfer-Williams Companies merger unfolds like an episode of “Seinfeld.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A farcical energy deal has given rise to a new takeover tactic. Call it the George Costanza defense. Like the character from the “Seinfeld” sitcom, Energy Transfer Equity wants to redefine the terms of a soured relationship: its $21 billion takeover of rival pipeline operator the Williams Companies. Energy Transfer, based in Dallas, aims to pay less cash and seems intent on forcing the issue by making itself as unattractive as possible. The stakes are no laughing matter, though.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/07/business/db-energyviews/db-energyviews-articleLarge.png', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/07/business/db-energyviews/db-energyviews-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/07/business/db-energyviews/db-energyviews-jumbo.png', 'height': 673, 'width': 958, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/07/business/db-energyviews/db-energyviews-super