e last year, redefining the meaning of being down and out and in the search for a place where one might live in relative safety. This context of human suffering made the ruling last week by Italy’s highest appeals court ring all the more with mercy in the case of a hungry Ukrainian immigrant facing jail time for stealing $4.70 worth of sausage and cheese in a Genoa supermarket.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mangia! Signore: Italian Court Spares Hungry Shoplifter', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mangia! Mangia! Signore Ostriakov', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-08T21:11:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4188bfee-5e31-526c-8490-a633058b86c5', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4188bfee-5e31-526c-8490-a633058b86c5'}, {'abstract': 'For a decade and a half, a rugged, fast-paced British offshoot of hip-hop has been remarkably durable, as two acts prove.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/09/arts/music/skepta-and-dizzee-rascal-illustrate-grimes-push-and-pull.html', 'snippet': 'For a decade and a half, a rugged, fast-paced British offshoot of hip-hop has been remarkably durable, as two acts prove.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a decade and a half, grime — the rugged, fast-paced British hip-hop offshoot — has been remarkably durable, one of England’s most vibrant and innovative music scenes, while also seeping out in fits and starts to the broader world. Whether grime is best when keeping to itself or engaging with everything else is a source of fervent fascination, questioning and debate, something as true now as it was at this genre’s inception. Unease about absorption into the mainstream is pervasive.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09GRIME1/09GRIME1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09GRIME1/09GRIME1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09GRIME1/09GRIME1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09GRIME1/09GRIME1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09GRIME1/09GRIME1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09GRIME1/09GRIME1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09GRIME1/09GRIME1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Skepta and Dizzee Rascal Illustrate Grime’s Push and Pull', 'kicker': "Critic's Notebook", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Artists Are Reminders of Grime’s Push and Pull', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skepta (1982- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dizzee Rascal (1984- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-08T21:15:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f27d914-3143-56dd-a499-21bb6d28fd23', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f27d914-3143-56dd-a499-21bb6d28fd23'}, {'abstract': 'If the government is to counter Islamic extremism, it must end its own crackdown on democratic freedoms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/09/opinion/bangladeshs-descent-into-lawlessness.html', 'snippet': 'If the government is to counter Islamic extremism, it must end its own crackdown on democratic freedoms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since 2013, more than 20 people in Bangladesh have been murdered by Islamist extremists, many hacked to death. Among the first victims were bloggers who had criticized Islamic fundamentalism. Then, two foreigners, an Italian aid worker and a Japanese farmer, were struck down last year. In just nine days last month, five people were hacked to death.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bangladesh’s Descent into Lawlessness', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bangladesh’s Descent into Lawlessness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangladesh', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wazed, Hasina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-08T21:18:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b2a722b-6a6f-517d-8cde-34cb8cb3c3b4', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b2a722b-6a6f-517d-8cde-34cb8cb3c3b4'}, {'abstract': 'The weather cooperated as 2,000 birds trained by the artist Duke Riley captivated onlookers during this performance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/09/arts/design/review-in-fly-by-night-pigeons-light-up-the-brooklyn-navy-yard.html', 'snippet': 'The weather cooperated as 2,000 birds trained by the artist Duke Riley captivated onlookers during this performance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From my experience, it seemed likely that “Fly by Night” — a performance by 2,000 pigeons conducted by the multitalented artist and bird maven Duke Riley in the Brooklyn Navy Yard — would be more fun to read about than to observe. This fate has befallen other public artworks in New York that relied on nature’s cooperation.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09PIGEONS/09PIGEONS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09PIGEONS/09PIGEONS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/09/arts/09PIGEONS/09PIGEONS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'ran