g', '_id': 'nyt://article/567ef30c-a7ea-5bc6-9d9f-5efe5d6543f5', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/567ef30c-a7ea-5bc6-9d9f-5efe5d6543f5'}, {'abstract': 'Dishes from Martha Washington in Manhattan’s oldest house.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/11/dining/hamilton-washington-cabinet-dinner-morris-jumel-mansion.html', 'snippet': 'Dishes from Martha Washington in Manhattan’s oldest house.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alexander Hamilton, Thomas Jefferson and John Adams dined with George Washington at his first cabinet dinner on July 12, 1790, at the Morris-Jumel Mansion in Upper Manhattan. The house, the oldest in Manhattan, was built in 1765 and served as Washington’s headquarters in the fall of 1776. It is now owned by the city, and on July 10, it will bring that cabinet dinner to life with a menu that includes Martha Washington’s lettuce and plum tarts, asparagus ragoo, dressed fish rémoulade, sausages, great cake and cider. Joanna Pruess, a culinary anthropologist, is in charge of the cooking: July 10, 6 to 8 p.m., $100 a person, 212-923-8008, morrisjumel.org/upcoming-events.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB/11BURNER-DINNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB/11BURNER-DINNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB/11BURNER-DINNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB/11BURNER-DINNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB/11BURNER-DINNER-WEB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Chance to Dine Like the Founding Fathers', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Dine: A Chance to Eat Like a Founding Father', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morris-Jumel Mansion (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adams, John (1735-1826)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamilton, Alexander', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jefferson, Thomas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Washington, George (1732-99)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-09T09:54:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/076ef956-fad7-5598-8a78-21107789c5af', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/076ef956-fad7-5598-8a78-21107789c5af'}, {'abstract': 'The Italian clothing store Domenico Vacca invites clients to get comfortable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/11/dining/domenico-vacca-nyc-espresso.html', 'snippet': 'The Italian clothing store Domenico Vacca invites clients to get comfortable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just steps from Fifth Avenue’s shopping bag scrum, you can sip a coffee in quiet luxury, perhaps nibbling a croissant or more substantial panini imagined by Cesare Casella, the consulting chef. The Italian haberdasher Domenico Vacca has outfitted his shiny new American flagship store with an espresso bar (and a barber shop, beauty salon and private club). When the wine license is approved, a late-afternoon stem of sangiovese will be available, too: DV Café, 15 West 55th Street, domenicovacca.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1358, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB/11BURNER-EXPRESSOBAR-WEB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Little Shopping, a Little Espresso', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Refresh: A Little Shopping, a Little Espresso', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Domenico Vacca (Fashion Label)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-09T09:55:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9069f0ee-b6fa-5672-9584-af7140b150f4', 'word_count': 79, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9069f0ee-b6fa-5672-9584-af7140b150f4'}, {'abstract': 'Rosemary’s offers a summer C.S.A. from its 40 acres in Dutchess County.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/11/dining/csa-rosemarys.html', 'snippet': 'Rosemary’s offers a summer C.S.A. from its 40 acres in Dutchess County.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A growing number of restaurants cultivate their own herbs, greens and so forth on terraces and, increasingly, outside the city. Blue Hill is the prime example; others include Narcissa, Blenheim and even P.J. Clarke’s, too. Now, in addition to its rooftop garden, Rosemary’s, along with its sister restaurants Claudette and Bobo, is tilling 40 acres near Fishkill, N.Y., in Dutchess County. What sets it apart is that it will sell its organic bounty, including eggs, flowers and optional fresh pasta, to the public in community supported agriculture shares: $400 for 20 weekly baskets from mid-June to mid-October, $240 for 10 biweekly baskets, to be picked up at Rosemary’s, 18 Greenwich Avenue (10th Street), rosemarysnyc.com/farm. Shares available until June 1.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-CSA/11BURNER-CSA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-CSA/11BURNER-CSA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-CSA/11BURNER-CSA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-CSA/11BURNER-CSA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-CSA/11BURNER-CSA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-CSA/11BURNER-CSA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-CSA/11BURNER-CSA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Farming for Restaurant Kitchens and Yours', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Prepare: Farming for Restaurants and Your Kitchen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Rosemary's (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-09T09:56:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0424b0cb-375a-5243-9325-5dd2eca4259e', 'word_count': 119, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0424b0cb-375a-5243-9325-5dd2eca4259e'}, {'abstract': 'Beef, pork and chicken sausages with Danish-style toppings at Grand Central Terminal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/11/dining/hot-dogs-grand-central-terminal-danish.html', 'snippet': 'Beef, pork and chicken sausages with Danish-style toppings at Grand Central Terminal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Along with opening a bakery, a high-end restaurant and, soon, a food hall, Claus Meyer has found time to carve out a hot-dog stand. It’s outside his restaurant, Agern, in Grand Central Terminal and it sells custom-made beef, pork and chicken sausages in natural casings that are sizzled on a griddle. They come smothered in such Danish-style trappings as onions, raw and fried; rémoulade; slaw; pickles; horseradish; crispy shallots; and ketchup. They are served on whole-grain buns. The chicken, called Hen Hound, needed a pile of toppings; the others had personality to spare. And if a hot dog is not what you want, there’s a companion deli next door that has Danish pastries, sandwiches, salads, candies heavy on licorice and other items like coffee, bread flour and vinegars: Danish Dogs, $7 to $8, Grand Central Terminal, 89 East 42nd Street.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1334, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB/11BURNER-HOTDOG-WEB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hot Dogs With Everything', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Lunch: Hot Dogs  With Everything', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Danish Dogs (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hot Dogs and Frankfurters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Grand Central Terminal (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meyer, Claus', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-09T09:57:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/418406eb-f186-5586-a815-3e46bcd4b67b', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/418406eb-f186-5586-a815-3e46bcd4b67b'}, {'abstract': 'On the rocks, with tonic or in a Negroni, to list just a few possibilities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/11/dining/malfy-gin-with-a-touch-of-lemon.html', 'snippet': 'On the rocks, with tonic or in a Negroni, to list just a few possibilities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I generally disdain flavored spirits. But I like this stylish Italian gin that borrows from limoncello and shines with tart, sunny lemon. Gin, after all, depends on added flavoring, including, sometimes, citrus peels. This one, from a family company that has been making gin in the Piedmont region of Italy for 40 years, infuses the alcohol with lemon peels pressed and added to the mix of juniper and other botanicals. It’s delightful on the rocks, with tonic, in a Negroni or instead of vodka in a slushy sgroppino whisked with lemon sorbet and prosecco: Malfy Gin, $33.99 at Columbus Ave. Wines & Spirits, 730 Columbus Avenue (96th Street), 212-865-7070, columbuswines.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-GIN/11BURNER-GIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 949, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-GIN/11BURNER-GIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 949}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-GIN/11BURNER-GIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 648, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-GIN/11BURNER-GIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1295, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-GIN/11BURNER-GIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-GIN/11BURNER-GIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/dining/11BURNER-GIN/11BURNER-GIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gin With a Touch of Lemon', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Sip: A Touch of Lemon  Goes With Gin', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gin (Liquor)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lemons', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Piedmont (Italy)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-09T09:59:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bf7fcb2-d081-5993-9f88-b9ddf61cb3e5', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bf7fcb2-d081-5993-9f88-b9ddf61cb3e5'}, {'abstract': 'Sometimes bacon doesn’t belong, but this combination works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/11/dining/chunky-pig-bacon-caramel-corn.html', 'snippet': 'Sometimes bacon doesn’t belong, but this combination works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bacon is sometimes featured in food and dishes where it does not belong. But this caramel corn welcomes it. It is riddled with bits of Niman Ranch bacon and a touch of chile for a fine snack, and it’s even better with peanuts. The San Mateo, Calif., company that makes it also folds the bacon corn into granola and coats it with dark chocolate: Chunky Pig Caramel Corn, $9, chunkypiglove.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 