'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Pérez-Peña and Tamar Lewin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pérez-Peña', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92aead00-4f45-52fd-b5f9-714f9d2f5ddd', 'word_count': 1126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92aead00-4f45-52fd-b5f9-714f9d2f5ddd'}, {'abstract': 'He has an opportunity to offer tangible initiatives to advance his vision of a nuclear-free world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/11/opinion/what-mr-obama-can-say-at-hiroshima.html', 'snippet': 'He has an opportunity to offer tangible initiatives to advance his vision of a nuclear-free world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama added a couple of firsts to his list of achievements when he became the first sitting president to visit Myanmar in 2012 and to visit Cuba in modern times. He will add another at the end of this month when he visits Hiroshima in conjunction with the Group of 7 leaders meeting in Japan. Though the White House is playing down expectations, the visit gives him a significant opportunity to offer some tangible new initiatives to advance his vision of a nuclear-free world — a major goal at the outset of his administration that has since faded against a host of other foreign policy challenges.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/11wed2web/11wed2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/11wed2web/11wed2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlarge