subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Howard University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Commencement Speeches', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-11T07:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/946e42a0-d50d-5148-81e8-d0fabd37507c', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/946e42a0-d50d-5148-81e8-d0fabd37507c'}, {'abstract': 'It would be better to teach Confucius alongside with Kant, but until then, we should face facts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/11/opinion/if-philosophy-wont-diversify-lets-call-it-what-it-really-is.html', 'snippet': 'It would be better to teach Confucius alongside with Kant, but until then, we should face facts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The vast majority of philosophy departments in the United States offer courses only on philosophy derived from Europe and the English-speaking world. For example, of the 118 doctoral programs in philosophy in the United States and Canada, only 10 percent have a specialist in Chinese philosophy as part of their regular faculty. Most philosophy departments also offer no courses on Africana, Indian, Islamic, Jewish, Latin American, Native American or other non-European traditions. Indeed, of the top 50 philosophy doctoral programs in the English-speaking world, only 15 percent have any regular faculty members who teach any non-Western philosophy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/09garfieldWeb/09garfieldWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 458, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/09garfieldWeb/09garfieldWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 458}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/09garfieldWeb/09garfieldWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 781, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/09garfieldWeb/09garfieldWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1563, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/09garfieldWeb/09garfieldWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/09garfieldWeb/09garfieldWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/09garfieldWeb/09garfieldWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'If Philosophy Won’t Diversify, Let’s Call It What It Really Is', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let’s Call Philosophy What It Really Is', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-11T07:21:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay L. Garfield and Bryan W. Van Norden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Garfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Bryan', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Van', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3cc7a22-87b7-527b-a2e1-f1a85d4019fa', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3cc7a22-87b7-527b-a2e1-f1a85d4019fa'}, {'abstract': 'In a first since the Watergate era, the Republican and Democratic nominating conventions will be bankrolled solely by corporations and wealthy individuals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/11/opinion/after-conventions-a-debt-to-donors.html', 'snippet': 'In a first since the Watergate era, the Republican and Democratic nominating conventions will be bankrolled solely by corporations and wealthy individuals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year the Republican and Democratic nominating conventions in Cleveland and Philadelphia will be bankrolled entirely with money from corporations and wealthy individuals. Not since the Watergate era, when a $400,000 pledge to the 1972 Republican convention from ITT Corporation was linked to a favorable outcome for the company in a federal antitrust decision, has this happened.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/11wed1/11wed1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/11wed1/11wed1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 627}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/11/opinion/11wed1/11wed1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 980, 'subType': 'jumb