one, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-05-11T07:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/680f5513-04f1-5a63-a14a-df3943a4cc3a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/680f5513-04f1-5a63-a14a-df3943a4cc3a'}, {'abstract': 'When Mr. Schmitz wrote his book on China, he looked to his daily surroundings in Shanghai rather than to some of the more exotic locales where he has reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/12/world/asia/q-and-a-rob-schmitz-on-shanghais-street-of-eternal-happiness.html', 'snippet': 'When Mr. Schmitz wrote his book on China, he looked to his daily surroundings in Shanghai rather than to some of the more exotic locales where he has reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rob Schmitz first came to China in 1996 as a Peace Corps volunteer assigned to Sichuan Province. In 2010, he settled in Shanghai as a correspondent for Marketplace, the American Public Media program. When he decided to write a book on China, it was to his daily surroundings in that city that he looked rather than to some of the more exotic locales where he has reported. “Street of Eternal Happiness: Big City Dreams Along a Shanghai Road” is about the people he came to know in the corner of the old French Concession where he and his family live.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/12/world/12chinaschmitz03/12chinaschmitz03-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/12/world/12chinaschmitz03/12chinaschmitz03-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':