cts', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Generic Brands and Products', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-11T23:29:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Strom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f870fa4-6cdb-51f5-924c-4cfe608b2770', 'word_count': 1083, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f870fa4-6cdb-51f5-924c-4cfe608b2770'}, {'abstract': 'A couple, played by Tim Daly and Rachael Holmes, are unsettled after one of them invites a guest to their home in this work, at City Center Stage II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/15/theater/penelope-skinners-the-ruins-of-civilization-looks-to-the-future.html', 'snippet': 'A couple, played by Tim Daly and Rachael Holmes, are unsettled after one of them invites a guest to their home in this work, at City Center Stage II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Penelope Skinner, the author of the well-received “The Village Bike,” seen in New York two years ago, comes the new play “The Ruins of Civilization.” Set in that ever-popular era — the future — the play concerns a couple, played by Tim Daly and Rachael Holmes, who are “living their married lives within the constraints and norms of a new world order,” as the news release cryptically puts it. Enter a mysterious guest, invited into their home by Ms. Holmes’s character in an impulsive act of charity that, perhaps not surprisingly, has unsettling results. Described as a thriller exploring our “need for free will and connection,” the play is directed by the Obie award winner Leah C. Gardiner for Manhattan Theater Club. (Now in previews and opening Wednesday, May 18, at City Center Stage II; 212-581-1212, nycitycenter.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE3/15GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 842, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE3/15GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 842}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE3/15GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 730, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE3/15GUIDE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1460, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE3/15GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE3/15GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE3/15GUIDE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Penelope Skinner’s ‘The Ruins of Civilization’ Looks to the Future', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theater: An Unsettled New World Order', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan Theater Club', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Ruins of Civilization (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skinner, Penelope', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardiner, Leah C', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daly, Tim', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holmes, Rachael (Actor)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-11T23:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06855aa1-9bd0-52b2-8aac-ec24fad0537e', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06855aa1-9bd0-52b2-8aac-ec24fad0537e'}, {'abstract': 'In New York City’s poorest borough, misdemeanor defendants wait months or years for a trial. A lawsuit aims to end that injustice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/12/opinion/a-nightmare-court-worthy-of-dickens.html', 'snippet': 'In New York City’s poorest borough, misdemeanor defendants wait months or years for a trial. A lawsuit aims to end that injustice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In September 2012, John Carridice, a father of four, intervened to break up a street fight in the Bronx. When the police showed up, they arrested him and charged him with resisting arrest, disorderly conduct and other misdemeanor violations.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/12/opinion/12thu1/12thu1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/12/opinion/12thu1/12thu1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': N