': 1537, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13eurovision/13eurovision-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13eurovision/13eurovision-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13eurovision/13eurovision-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spain’s Eurovision Entry, in a First, Is Sung Entirely in English', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spanish Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-12T08:52:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raphael Minder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raphael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Minder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74142146-1f50-5808-8d7a-1cb68424bff0', 'word_count': 578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74142146-1f50-5808-8d7a-1cb68424bff0'}, {'abstract': 'At a Catholic nursing home in New York, comforting patients who are dying of cancer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/15/magazine/the-sisters-who-treat-the-untreatable.html', 'snippet': 'At a Catholic nursing home in New York, comforting patients who are dying of cancer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the beginning of the 20th century, sick Americans typically died at home. By the middle of it, they mostly died in hospitals. And yet this great transformation in the geography of death was, at first, of little interest to medical providers: In the 1960s, some doctors routinely chose not to inform terminal patients of their fate. Studies found hospitals stashing dying people at the ends of halls and largely ignoring them. Medicine, it was said, was about healing people. It had nothing to offer the already dying.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '68', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB/15nuns-ss-slide-Y3YB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sisters Who Treat the Untreatable', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Standard of Care', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Laub, Gillian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuns', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospice Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Laub, Gillian (1975- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-12T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Photographs by Gillian Laub and Brooke Jarvis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Photographs', 'middlename': 'Gillian', 'lastname': 'Laub', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jarvis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33f01d53-ccbe-5252-90e1-61bf6b3abd15', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33f01d53-ccbe-5252-90e1-61bf6b3abd15'}, {'abstract': 'In an era of rapidly proliferating, precisely targeted treatments, every cancer case has to be played by ear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/15/magazine/oncologist-improvisation.html', 'snippet': 'In an era of rapidly proliferating, precisely targeted treatments, every cancer case has to be played by ear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The bone-marrow biopsy took about 20 minutes. It was 10 o’clock on an unusually chilly morning in New York in April, and Donna M., a self-possessed 78-year-old woman, had flown in from Chicago to see me in my office at Columbia University Medical Center. She had treated herself to orchestra seats for “The Humans” the night before, and was now waiting in the room as no one should be asked to wait: pants down, spine curled, knees lifted to her chest — a grown woman curled like a fetus. I snapped on sterile gloves while the nurse pulled out a bar cart containing a steel needle the length of an index finger. The rim of Donna’s pelvic bone was numbed with a pulse of anesthetic, and I drove the needle, as gently as I could, into the outer furl of bone. A corkscrew of pain spiraled through her body as the marrow was pulled, and then a few milliliters of red, bone-flecked sludge filled the syringe. It was slightly viscous, halfway between liquid and gel, like the crushed pulp of an overripe strawberry.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '43', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15oncologist1/15oncologist1-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15oncologist1/15oncologist1-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 700}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15oncologist1/15oncologist1-jumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 878, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15oncologist1/15oncologist1-superJumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 1715, 'width': 1470, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15oncologist1/15mag-15oncologist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15oncologist1/15mag-15oncologist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15oncologist1/15mag-15oncologist-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Improvisational Oncologist', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doctor Without Borders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mukherjee, Siddhartha', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clinical Trials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemotherapy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University Medical Center', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-12T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siddhartha Mukherjee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siddhartha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mukherjee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be8565b0-08c2-54a1-8a03-aebd80d84f6e', 'word_count': 4690, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be8565b0-08c2-54a1-8a03-aebd80d84f6e'}, {'abstract': 'In the early 20th century, the German biochemist Otto Warburg believed that tumors could be treated by disrupting their source of energy. His idea was dismissed for decades — until now.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/15/magazine/warburg-effect-an-old-idea-revived-starve-cancer-to-death.html', 'snippet': 'In the early 20th century, the German biochemist Otto Warburg believed that tumors could be treated by disrupting their source of energy. His idea was dismissed for decades — until now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The story of modern cancer research begins, somewhat improbably, with the sea urchin. In the first decade of the 20th century, the German biologist Theodor Boveri discovered that if he fertilized sea-urchin eggs with two sperm rather than one, some of the cells would end up with the wrong number of chromosomes and fail to develop properly. It was the era before modern genetics, but Boveri was aware that cancer cells, like the deformed sea urchin cells, had abnormal chromosomes; whatever caused cancer, he surmised, had something to do with chromosomes.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '64', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15warburg1-promo/15mag-15warburg-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 738, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15warburg1-promo/15mag-15warburg-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 738}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15warburg1-promo/15mag-15warburg-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 833, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/magazine/15warburg1-promo/15mag-15warburg-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1666, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit