 Zeus,” truly is a wonder: a trove of ancient artifacts, excavated over some four decades, from a city that flourished on the lower slopes of Mount Olympus in northern Greece under the Macedonian king Archelaus I, great-grandfather of Alexander the Great.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1408, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Gods and Mortals at Olympus,’ Right There on Fifth Avenue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art: Olympian Gods on Fifth Avenue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Onassis Cultural Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greek Civilization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Gods and Mortals at Olympus: Ancient Dion, City of Zeus (Exhibit)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-12T21:02:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/689d35e6-36d2-5ebd-af8d-861d4900bd9c', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/689d35e6-36d2-5ebd-af8d-861d4900bd9c'}, {'abstract': 'The tour, at the Barclays Center, will include Mase, Total, Faith Evans, Mary J. Blige, Lil’ Kim and Jay Z.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/15/arts/music/bad-boy-family-reunion-recalls-1990s-new-york-hip-hop.html', 'snippet': 'The tour, at the Barclays Center, will include Mase, Total, Faith Evans, Mary J. Blige, Lil’ Kim and Jay Z.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The hole at the center of New York hip-hop — the 1997 death of the Notorious B.I.G. — will be the implicit foundation for the Bad Boy Family Reunion, which is coming to the Barclays Center for a pair of sold-out shows. Bad Boy Records defined mainstream New York hip-hop in the 1990s and also helped create the template for the genre’s pop future — even if the label is far from its peak now, its lessons have been so assimilated as to be ubiquitous.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad Boy Family Reunion Recalls 1990s New York Hip-Hop', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop: New York Bad Boys Plan a Reunion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bad Boy Entertainment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, Christopher G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays Center (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-12T21:02:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a5a8a02-3437-5d22-8f6d-5552a07ebcde', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a5a8a02-3437-5d22-8f6d-5552a07ebcde'}, {'abstract': 'The comedian gets a second special, on May 20.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/15/arts/television/brad-williams-returns-to-showtime-with-daddy-issues.html', 'snippet': 'The comedian gets a second special, on May 20.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Own your differences, the empowerment mantra says. No one is doing that more hilariously than Brad Williams, who gets his second Showtime comedy special on Friday, May 20. Mr. Williams uses his dwarfism both for self-deprecation and to expose the daily prejudices and misconceptions he encounters. “Here’s how I write jokes,” he said in his first Showtime special, “Fun Size,” last year. “Step 1: Be a dwarf. Step 2: Wait.” In the new special, “Daddy Issues,” he serves up a rollicking story about running a race in the Special Olympics and chastises the men in the audience over which urinal not to use in a public bathroom. And he finishes the set with anecdotes about his father that are both funny and penetrating. “I’m so thankful that he put a little adversity in my life,” he says. “That way I knew how to conquer adversity later. You have to ask yourself one question: Who do you want your kid to be? Do you want your kid to be ready for the world that is, or for the world that should be?”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Brad Williams Returns to Showtime With ‘Daddy Issues’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Television: This Comic Owns His Differences', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Showtime', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Brad (1984- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Daddy Issues (TV Program)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-12T21:03:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8c6609f-0b5e-53d1-9613-eb083fd05677', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8c6609f-0b5e-53d1-9613-eb083fd05677'}, {'abstract': 'A survey of the Brazilian landscape architect, whose designs include the promenade along the Rio de Janeiro beachfront, is at the Jewish Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/13/arts/design/review-revisiting-the-constructed-edens-of-roberto-burle-marx.html', 'snippet': 'A survey of the Brazilian landscape architect, whose designs include the promenade along the Rio de Janeiro beachfront, is at the Jewish Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You get back to Eden by preserving what exists of the original, or by creating new versions of your own. Roberto Burle Marx, the great Brazilian landscape architect, did both. From the 1930s onward, mainly in South America, he designed some of the modern world’s most distinctive parks and gardens, from an immense, jazzy tattoo of a promenade on the Rio de Janeiro beachfront to rooftop plantings in a city carved overnight from jungle, Brasília. In the process, he became deeply invested, heart and mind, in saving the Amazonian paradise that surrounded him; fought to halt its devastation; and turned his home near Rio into a botanical rescue farm, a sanctuary for one of the world’s largest collections of tropical plants.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13ROBERTO/13ROBERTO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13ROBERTO/13ROBERTO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13ROBERTO/13ROBERTO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13ROBERTO/13ROBERTO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1359, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13ROBERTO/13ROBERTO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13ROBERTO/13ROBERTO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13ROBERTO/13ROBERTO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting the Constructed Edens of Roberto Burle Marx', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Creating Edens, Tropical and Sensual', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'ke