person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/26ac0f94-dbee-59e1-9a15-784b835df85b', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26ac0f94-dbee-59e1-9a15-784b835df85b'}, {'abstract': 'Chronicling a 1,200-mile pilgrimage by the Buddhist residents of a Tibetan village, this movie upends the traditions of documentary and narrative filmmaking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/13/movies/review-paths-of-the-soul-a-road-trip-unlike-any-other.html', 'snippet': 'Chronicling a 1,200-mile pilgrimage by the Buddhist residents of a Tibetan village, this movie upends the traditions of documentary and narrative filmmaking.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Paths of the Soul” is rare in several respects, not least that it is a depiction of Buddhism in Tibet that has passed muster with Chinese censors. Directed by Zhang Yang, it dramatizes a 1,200-mile pilgrimage by the actual residents of the Tibetan village of Nyima. The movie so upends the traditions of documentary and narrative filmmaking that “dramatizes” may be inaccurate — the filmmakers followed the real pilgrims for a full year, after all. But the movie is so well made and engaging that such distinctions will make little difference to the viewer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13PATHS/13PATHS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13PATHS/13PATHS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13PATHS/13PATHS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13PATHS/13PATHS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13PATHS/13PATHS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13PATHS/13PATHS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13PATHS/13PATHS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Paths of the Soul,’ a Road Trip Unlike Any Other', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Review: ‘Paths of the Soul,’ a Road Trip Unlike Any Other', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zhang Yang', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Paths of the Soul (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-12T21:42:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Kenny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/620f2997-9129-56ae-bc58-ad4d847964af', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/620f2997-9129-56ae-bc58-ad4d847964af'}, {'abstract': 'This Soi Cheang film, involving a principled prison guard with an ill daughter, has plot and subplot, but the action scenes are most notable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/13/movies/review-kill-zone-2-packs-a-wallop-with-orchestrated-mayhem.html', 'snippet': 'This Soi Cheang film, involving a principled prison guard with an ill daughter, has plot and subplot, but the action scenes are most notable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there’s one rewarding thing about many Hong Kong action directors, it’s that they rarely dawdle in getting to what fight fans have come for: bracing shootouts and high-impact fisticuffs and footwork. Soi Cheang, a former assistant to the likes of the Hong Kong auteurs Ringo Lam and Johnnie To, honors this tradition with the generically titled policier “Kill Zone 2,” a quasi-sequel to a 2005 Donnie Yen vehicle and a hearty, layered, big-budget helping of orchestrated mayhem.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13KILLZONE2/13KILLZONE2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13KILLZONE2/13KILLZONE2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13KILLZONE2/13KILLZONE2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13KILLZONE2/13KILLZONE2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13KILLZONE2/13KILLZONE2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/13/arts/13KILLZONE2/13KILLZONE2-thumbStan