: 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-13T07:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8711acbc-412a-5a6c-ab2c-f9adc393339c', 'word_count': 18, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8711acbc-412a-5a6c-ab2c-f9adc393339c'}, {'abstract': 'Donald Trump, good at reality TV and developing buildings, is cocksure he can run the free world. But in politics there are no instant masters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/13/opinion/the-one-minute-life.html', 'snippet': 'Donald Trump, good at reality TV and developing buildings, is cocksure he can run the free world. But in politics there are no instant masters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m sure a sizable number of people cheered the finding that 60 seconds of strenuous exercise may be just as beneficial as a sweaty 45 minutes. Who needs to run through the woods, cycle across the city or pound the hamster wheel at the gym when you can get the same results in less time than it takes to microwave a burrito?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The One-Minute Life', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gladwell, Malcolm', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-13T07:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/85fc28c3-04ce-5abf-b9b1-5fd5e45a0691', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85fc28c3-04ce-5abf-b9b1-5fd5e45a0691'}, {'abstract': 'State lawmakers need to stop playing games and let Mayor Bill de Blasio keep control over city schools.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/13/opinion/albany-plays-the-schoolyard-bully.html', 'snippet': 'State lawmakers need to stop playing games and let Mayor Bill de Blasio keep control over city schools.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Republicans in the New York State Senate are eager to punish Mayor Bill de Blasio for campaigning against them two years ago in a failed attempt to restore Democratic control in the Legislature. Grudges like this are a natural part of politics. But it would be irresponsible of lawmakers to punish New York City’s 1.1 million schoolchildren as a way of settling a score with Mr. de Blasio.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/opinion/13fri3web/13fri3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/13/opinion/13fri3web/13fri3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/opinion/13fri3web/13fri3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 653, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/opinion/13fri3web/13fri3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1306, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/opinion/13fri3web/13fri3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/13/opinion/13fri3web/13fri3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/13/opinion/13fri3web/13fri3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Albany Plays the Schoolyard Bully', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Albany Plays the Schoolyard Bully', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-13T07:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1bab819-71de-5741-b68e-408ab21fbb95', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1bab819-71de-5741-b68e-408ab21fbb95'}, {'abstract': 'His personal returns, his shifting policy proposals and how he picked his experts are all mysterious.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/13/opinion/trump-and-taxes.html', 'snippet': 'His personal returns, his shifting policy proposals and how he picked his experts are all mysterious.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This seems to be the week for Trump tax mysteries. One mystery is why Donald Trump, unlike every other major party nominee in modern times, is refusing to release his tax returns. The other is why, having decided that he needs experts to clean up his ludicrous tax-cut proposals, he chose to call on the services of the gang that couldn’t think straight.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 591, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 591}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/kru