'nyt://slideshow/9589fe8e-f965-5e35-b243-f2d0c8463296'}, {'abstract': 'Dean Wong’s new book on Chinese-American communities is a powerful corrective to decades of reporting on neighborhoods often represented in the cultural mainstream as exotic, insular or irrelevant.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/05/16/why-chinatown-still-matters/', 'snippet': 'Dean Wong’s new book on Chinese-American communities is a powerful corrective to decades of reporting on neighborhoods often represented in the cultural mainstream as exotic, insular or irrelevant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is one thing we do not see in a compelling 1982 self-portrait by Dean Wong: his face. Taken in Seattle’s Chinatown, the photograph zeroes in on the back of a metal helmet, polished to a mirrorlike finish. In it is reflected a crowd of neighborhood residents — a metaphor for the people and hometown community that have shaped and fascinated Mr. Wong. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Chinatown Still Matters', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wong, Dean (Photographer)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-16T07:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maurice Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maurice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a66467fb-e34d-5b67-ae38-54eca883efe6', 'word_count': 988, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a66467fb-e34d-5b67-ae38-54eca883efe6'}, {