ards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3370e2fa-9f85-56e1-b1b2-090be00eacea', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3370e2fa-9f85-56e1-b1b2-090be00eacea'}, {'abstract': 'What are the odds of getting two heads in a row if you flip a coin N times?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/05/16/oconnor-two-heads/', 'snippet': 'What are the odds of getting two heads in a row if you flip a coin N times?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our puzzle this week was suggested by\xa0Mike O’Connor, a retired software engineer. “The puzzle was given to me by my friend Jon Stein,” explained Mike by email. “I stumbled on it for a little bit and it reminded me of a puzzle you had last year by Steve Humble [see “Steve Humble’s Neighbor Problem”]. Both puzzles sent me down wrong paths of realizing my logic didn’t anticipate events that weren’t actually independent. It seems like an easy question but the solution actually isn’t obvious. It turns out it has a really simple elegant solution.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Heads in a Row', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-16T16:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Antonick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Antonick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05c18616-8413-5968-9e2f-a5e65a44e7e2', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05c18616-8413-5968-9e2f-a5e65a44e7e2'