 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/16/nytnow/16eveningss-slide-4LAT/16eveningss-slide-4LAT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/16/nytnow/16eveningss-slide-4LAT/16eveningss-slide-4LAT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/16/nytnow/16eveningss-slide-4LAT/16eveningss-slide-4LAT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Evening Briefing: Hillary Clinton, Donald Trump, Cultural Revolution', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Your Evening Briefing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-05-16T21:59:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'NYT Now', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrea Kannapell and Sandra Stevenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kannapell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/769cd153-7576-5cdb-b481-b2dc7c9d78ca', 'word_count': 799, 'uri': 'nyt://article/769cd153-7576-5cdb-b481-b2dc7c9d78ca'}, {'abstract': 'In “Shelter From the Storm,” the title of her new album and her show at Joe’s Pub, Ms. Jungr showed that she can squeeze more juice out of a Dylan song than just about anybody.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/17/arts/music/barb-jungrs-jazzy-songs-for-troubled-times.html', 'snippet': 'In “Shelter From the Storm,” the title of her new album and her show at Joe’s Pub, Ms. Jungr showed that she can squeeze more juice out of a Dylan song than just about anybody.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the British singer Barb Jungr was growing up in Manchester, she recalled on Saturday evening, the images on an album cover of “South Pacific” gave her the illusion that a tropical island was a safe, paradisiacal refuge. At Joe’s Pub, where she is performing songs from her new album, “Shelter From the Storm: Songs of Hope for Troubled Times,” Ms. Jungr, in a green silk shirt, shell necklace and black slacks, appeared as Bloody Mary and sang “Bali Hai” with a trio that included the piano virtuoso Laurence Hobgood, Wilson Torres on percussion and Matt Clohesy on bass. These brilliant musicians lifted the performance out of the realm of cabaret and into jazz.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/17/arts/17JUNGER/17JUNGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/17/arts/17JUNGER/17JUNGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/17/arts/17JUNGER/17JUNGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 605, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/17/arts/17JUNGER/17JUNGER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1210, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/17/arts/17JUNGER/17JUNGER-thumbStandard