a Twin Peaks restaurant at a meeting of a regional coalition of motorcycle clubs after an altercation between two rival groups, the Bandidos and the Cossacks. So many bikers were arrested — nearly 200 — that officials used the Waco Convention Center for initial processing.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/us/18waco1/18waco1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/18/us/18waco1/18waco1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/us/18waco1/18waco1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 662, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/us/18waco1/18waco1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1324, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/us/18waco1/18waco1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/18/us/18waco1/18waco1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/us/18waco1/18waco1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One Year After Shootout, Waco’s Bikers Struggle to Move On', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Year After Shootout, Waco’s Bikers Try to Move Forward', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Montgomery, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Waco (Tex)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fernandez, Manny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gangs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Motorcycles, Motor Bikes and Motorscooters', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cossacks Motorcycle Club', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bandidos Motorcycle Club', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Imprisonments', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-18T01:25:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manny Fernandez and David Montgomery', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Montgomery', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f15ff5a-879c-5db6-b30e-1315c56931de', 'word_count': 1274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f15ff5a-879c-5db6-b30e-1315c56931de'}, {'abstract': 'News and updates from around the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/18/us/national-briefing.html', 'snippet': 'News and updates from around the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Chicago police officer whose fatal shooting of an unarmed woman was one in a series of killings of young black people that shattered the public’s trust has quit rather than fight to keep his job. The city’s police board announced Tuesday that the officer, Dante Servin, who was off duty when he shot Rekia Boyd in 2012, resigned two days before a hearing at which the board was to decide if he should be fired. Ms. Boyd, 22, was walking with friends on a street when Mr. Servin told them to be quiet. He said he had fired because he had seen a person moving toward him with a gun, but the police found only a cellphone at the scene. He was charged with involuntary manslaughter, but a judge acquitted him, saying Mr. Servin had been improperly charged, and suggested that if the act was intentional, then the crime, “if any there be, is first-degree murder.” The city settled a lawsuit in 2013 with the Boyd family for $4.5 million. (AP)', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'National Briefing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'National Briefing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voter Registration and Requirements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-18T01:40:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfddb41d-2041-5284-9ebe-fbab3b5de4ed', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfddb41d-2041-5284-9ebe-fbab3b5de4ed'}, {'abstract': 'This powerful new drama by Paula Vogel sheds light on a time when history, Jewish culture and the depiction of lesbian love explosively intersected.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/18/theater/review-indecent-revisits-a-play-colliding-with-broadway-mores-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'This powerful new drama by Paula Vogel sheds light on a time when history, Jewish culture and the depiction of lesbian love explosively intersected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The protagonist of “Indecent,” the powerful new play by Paula Vogel, is not a person but a drama — meaning that this is a play as much about the fate of a play as it is about the lives of the people involved in it. This superbly realized production, created by Ms. Vogel and the director Rebecca Taichman, charts the tumultuous fortunes of “God of Vengeance,” a work by Sholem Asch that caused a scandal when it was produced on Broadway in 1923.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18INDECENT/18INDECENT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18INDECENT/18INDECENT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18INDECENT/18INDECENT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18INDECENT/18INDECENT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1303, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18INDECENT/18INDECENT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18INDECENT/18INDECENT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18INDECENT/18INDECENT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Indecent’ Revisits a Play Colliding With Broadway Mores and More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': ' A Drama Consumed by the Fires of History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vogel, Paula', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taichman, Rebecca Bayla', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Asch, Sholem', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vineyard Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Indecent (Play)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'God of Vengeance (Play)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-18T02:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dbdc858-a06e-5bd4-93ca-85d9491784ff', 'word_count': 1036, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dbdc858-a06e-5bd4-93ca-85d9491784ff'}, {'abstract': 'David Hare’s drama shows Wilde before and after his imprisonment for homosexuality, illustrating love as a force both sacred and profane.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/18/theater/review-rupert-everett-brings-oscar-wilde-to-the-stage-in-the-judas-kiss.html', 'snippet': 'David Hare’s drama shows Wilde before and after his imprisonment for homosexuality, illustrating love as a force both sacred and profane.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let us now bend our knees before the shrine of St. Oscar, and say a prayer to one who not only gave the English language perfect epigrams but also, it seems, died for our sins. That would be Oscar Wilde — the poet, playwright and bon vivant, who in the face of unrelenting persecution remained dauntlessly witty, impeccably stylish and morally fixed.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18JUDAS1/18JUDAS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18JUDAS1/18JUDAS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18JUDAS1/18JUDAS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18JUDAS1/18JUDAS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18JUDAS1/18JUDAS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18JUDAS1/18JUDAS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/18/arts/18JUDAS1/18JUDAS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Rupert Everett Brings Oscar Wilde to the Stage in ‘The Judas Kiss’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Society’s Punishment for a Forbidden Love, Taken Sitting Down', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hare, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMFIELD, NEIL', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilde, Oscar', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Judas Kiss (Play)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Everett, Rupert', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-18T02:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/112cf1c1-e2e4-52f5-93b9-21a4b6ff74c4', 'word_count': 1042, 'uri': 'nyt://article/112cf1c1-e2e4-52f5-93b9-21a4b6ff74c4'}, {'abstract': 'Zhouqin “C.C.” Burnikel finds something of note in today’s theme.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/05/17/where-the-midnight-sun-can-be-observed/', 'snippet': 'Zhouqin “C.C.” Burnikel finds something of note in today’s theme.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEDNESDAY PUZZLE — There are those people who prefer not to see letters spelled out in crosswords — like EFFS for Fs — but my theory is that, if you wait long enough, there will always be some constructor who finds a way to make it acceptable. Today, Zhouqin Burnikel has done just that. And she’s taken it a step further, so that her theme hits you like a one-two punch.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where the Midnight Sun Can Be Observed', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, '