rticle/65f059b4-94dd-59c0-af9e-e1e102b7f024', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65f059b4-94dd-59c0-af9e-e1e102b7f024'}, {'abstract': 'Checking out the in-car-entertainment value of nonfiction best sellers adapted for young readers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/22/books/review/the-omnivores-dilemma-unbroken-and-more-adapted-for-young-readers.html', 'snippet': 'Checking out the in-car-entertainment value of nonfiction best sellers adapted for young readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I still remember reading books to my own kids, teenagers now, but I don’t remember the last time someone read me a book, or even a paragraph, other than my husband barking out a snippet of the day’s outrageous news. Yet I’ve never forgotten how different the experience of listening to prose is from reading or watching it transformed into film. It requires time and a mental stillness, the kind one has these days mainly in cars or other modes of transit. And so I set out to listen to the audio of young readers’ versions of best-selling nonfiction in the car on trips upstate, often with my 13-year-old along to test their Y.A. appeal.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/books/review/22BURLEIGH/22BURLEIGH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 320, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/books/review/22BURLEIGH/22BURLEIGH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 320}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/books/review/22BURLEIGH/22BURLEIGH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 547, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/books/review/22BURLEIGH/22BURL