name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-18T21:26:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbd3f21c-b553-538d-8fbd-e260b55c8aa1', 'word_count': 1666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbd3f21c-b553-538d-8fbd-e260b55c8aa1'}, {'abstract': 'In his new show, “The Total Bent,” the music is always the play, and vice versa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/22/theater/whats-new-for-stew-a-blues-fable-told-in-story-and-song.html', 'snippet': 'In his new show, “The Total Bent,” the music is always the play, and vice versa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The playwright sat wearing, and occasionally playing, a red Epiphone guitar that was wirelessly connected to an amp with a light touch of reverb, as scenes from his new show unfolded in a Brooklyn rehearsal room. A music stand in front of him held not music, but his script — though the distinction may be moot. For Stew, the singer/songwriter/bandleader who won a Tony in 2008 for the book of his maiden theatrical effort, “Passing Strange,” the music is always the play, and vice versa.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22TOTALBENT2/22TOTALBENT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22TOTALBENT2/22TOTALBENT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22TOTALBENT2/22TOTALBENT2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22TOTALBENT2/22TOTALBENT2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22TOTALBENT2/22TOTALBENT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22TOTALBENT2/22TOTALBENT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22TOTALBENT2/22TOTALBENT2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s New for Stew? A Blues Fable Told in Story and Song', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Story Is the Song, and Vice Versa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stew (1961- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Settle, Joanna', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Passing Strange (Play)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Total Bent (Play)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-18T21:33:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Weinert-Kendt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weinert-Kendt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b810b187-d3f6-54ae-ab6d-eaa74adf911b', 'word_count': 1174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b810b187-d3f6-54ae-ab6d-eaa74adf911b'}, {'abstract': 'A new nostalgic design for the global traveler.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/18/t-magazine/fashion/jaquet-droz-twelve-cities-watch.html', 'snippet': 'A new nostalgic design for the global traveler.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 18th-century Swiss clockmaker Pierre Jaquet-Droz was famous for his technically elaborate creations — many of which incorporated music and other sophisticated complications. The company that still bears his name has now channeled his engineering prowess into a highly intricate timepiece with a startlingly spare design. The Twelve Cities watch — with a rose gold case and an ivory-hued face created in the ancient grand feu technique, in which thin layers of enamel are baked at extremely high temperatures — is aimed at a nostalgic world traveler. The hour is displayed as a numeral through an aperture at the top of the dial, and the wearer can customize the watch by choosing a favorite city in 12 different time zones. By depressing a pusher, the timepiece can be synced to any of those destinations, its name glimpsed through a second aperture reminiscent of the classic ticking departure boards of Europe’s grand train stations.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/t-magazine/22tmag-05qual_thing_watch-t_CA0/22tmag-05qual_thing_watch-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1006, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/t-magazine/22tmag-05qual_thing_watch-t_CA0/22tmag-05qual_thing_watch-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xl