, {'abstract': 'Alexei Ratmansky’s work, presented by American Ballet Theater, remains a fascinating, thrilling, bewilderingly ambiguous evocation of life in Shostakovich’s Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/19/arts/dance/review-shostakovich-trilogy-where-joy-and-grief-flourish.html', 'snippet': 'Alexei Ratmansky’s work, presented by American Ballet Theater, remains a fascinating, thrilling, bewilderingly ambiguous evocation of life in Shostakovich’s Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first, frenzied moments of Alexei Ratmansky’s “Shostakovich Trilogy,” a man hurls his body through the air horizontally, trusting other men to catch him. It’s a jaunty, carefree gesture, befitting a brass fanfare in the score. But it is characteristic of how the Russian-born Mr. Ratmansky hears the music of this great Russian composer that the act also feels reckless, foolish. The leaping man is caught, but later, people are dropped.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/19/arts/19SHOSTAKOVICH/19SHOSTAKOVICH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/19/arts/19SHOSTAKOVICH/19SHOSTAKOVICH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/19/arts/19SHOSTAKOVICH/19SHOSTAKOVICH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/19/arts/19SHOSTAKOVICH/19SHOSTAKOVICH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1345, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '