 SARAH ALMUKHTAR and DEREK WATKINS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ALMUKHTAR', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Derek', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'WATKINS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/8085bd7b-f390-5d87-98e3-427e6e13d622', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/8085bd7b-f390-5d87-98e3-427e6e13d622'}, {'abstract': 'News and updates from around the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/19/us/national-briefing.html', 'snippet': 'News and updates from around the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Under fire from the governor and many Republican legislators, the state Department of Education now says it will not follow new federal guidance on use of bathrooms and locker rooms by transgender students. State Superintendent Carey Wright announced Wednesday that the department would “follow the lead of state leadership” and take no action until the state Board of Education could discuss the situation. The move comes as Republicans in a number of other states have opposed the guidance, with some seeking to join legal challenges. Mississippi education officials had said Friday they would follow the guidance by federal authorities calling for transgender students to be treated consistently with their gender identity. They cited a need for a “safe and caring school environment.” The State Board of Education chairman, John Kelly of Gulfport, said that board would have a special meeting within the next two weeks to discuss the issue. The move came as opposition to Ms. Wright’s earlier announcement that she would comply turned into a landslide. Wednesday, 27 Republican state senators among the 32-member Republican supermajority sent a letter to her and the Board of Education calling for “swift and decisive action on this urgent matter.” (AP)', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'National Briefing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'National Briefing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transgender and Transsexuals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathrooms and Toilets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ducey, Doug (1964- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Aspen (Colo)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Affordable Housing', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-18T23:33:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e637110-6b5f-5481-b190-1d00485d6a31', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e637110-6b5f-5481-b190-1d00485d6a31'}, {'abstract': 'The prime minister, whose talks with the Zionist Union broke down, has offered to name Avigdor Lieberman, leader of the Yisrael Beiteinu party, defense minister.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/19/world/middleeast/benjamin-netanyahu-israel-lieberman-nationalism.html', 'snippet': 'The prime minister, whose talks with the Zionist Union broke down, has offered to name Avigdor Lieberman, leader of the Yisrael Beiteinu party, defense minister.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — In a sharp turnaround, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu sought on Wednesday to bring the ultranationalist Yisrael Beiteinu party into his governing coalition