books/review/programmed-obsolescence.html', 'snippet': 'Incorporating up-to-date devices and applications in fiction risks mayfly ephemerality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Jonathan Franzen’s “The Corrections,” published in 2001, one of the adult Lambert siblings, Gary, takes a laptop to his basement to find some information on a corporation and its founder. “Feeling a bit e-weary, fighting an e-headache, he ran a word search for earl eberle,” Franzen writes. Gary next finds a document in an S.E.C. database and hits the “Download Later button.” Then, as unattended men at subterranean computers are wont to do, Gary searches for “beautiful, nude and blond” and is treated to a series of lewd images.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Programmed Obsolescence', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Take', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Programmed Obsolescence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franzen, Jonathan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joyce, James', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-19T09:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teddy Wayne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teddy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wayne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77fb96c5-864a-5812-b7eb-4c378aa3236b', 'word_count': 917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77fb96c5-864a-5812-b7eb-4c378aa3236b'}, {'abstract': 'The all-stock deal, worth $13 billion, would combine the American and French companies, which have been hit hard by the global plunge in 