e New York Philharmonic fires the opening salvos in its new-music Biennial, other ensembles invite listeners to travel back in time with out-of-the-ordinary programs in out-of-the-way places. On Tuesday, May 24, the Salon Sanctuary series tunnels deep into the past with an evening of pre-modern West African chants and dances. The Liberian-born soprano Dawn Padmore leads a lineup of scholar-performers that includes the drummer Anicet Mundundu; the multi-instrumentalist Kevin Nathaniel Hylton; and the kora player Yacouba Sissoko, an heir to a notable family of storytellers from Mali. (Bernie Wohl Center in Manhattan, Salonsanctuary.org.) On Thursday, Midtown Concerts offers a refuge from the lunch-hour rush as the period-instrument string ensemble Acronym and the elegant baritone Jesse Blumberg perform cantatas by the 17th-century composer and hymn-smith Johann Rosenmüller. (Chapel of St. Bartholomew’s Church in Manhattan, Midtownconcerts.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1311, 'width': 1311, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Salon Sanctuary and Midtown Concerts Offer Early Music', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classical; Back in Time Across Globe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Salon Sanctuary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-19T19:47:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19cf87e1-a7ce-5b1e-92b0-71b44837ded4', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19cf87e1-a7ce-5b1e-92b0-71b44837ded4'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Mendelsohn’s character returns to haunt just about everybody.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/22/arts/television/bloodline-returns-to-netflix-for-season-2.html', 'snippet': 'Ben Mendelsohn’s character returns to haunt just about everybody.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Danny Rayburn is dead, but he’s not buried. That’s great news for the Ben Mendelsohn fans who were worried that the most heartbreakingly divisive character on Netflix’s “Bloodline” would vanish with the Florida Keys’ changing tides after his younger brother killed him.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Bloodline’ Returns to Netflix for Season 2', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Televison; Blood Still Tells on a Netflix Show', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bloodline (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mendelsohn, Ben (1969- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-19T19:49:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddab12d7-8933-5a60-b824-dbe361313bd6', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddab12d7-8933-5a60-b824-dbe361313bd6'}, {'abstract': '“The Brothers Le Nain: Painters of Seventeenth-Century France” illuminates one of the greatest, least-known sources of modern realism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/22/arts/design/le-nain-brothers-prescient-work-at-kimbell-art-museum.html', 'snippet': '“The Brothers Le Nain: Painters of Seventeenth-Century France” illuminates one of the greatest, least-known sources of modern realism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the least-known sources of modern realism will be honored Sunday, May 22, in Fort Worth, where the Kimbell Art Museum is opening “The Brothers Le Nain: Painters of Seventeenth-Century France.” Contemporaries of Velazquez and Georges de La Tour, the brothers — Antoine, Louis and Mathieu — were prominent in Paris in the 1630s and 1640s. Often signing their efforts simply Le Nain, they elevated genre painting with dignified depictions of peasants usually seated, motionless, in landscapes or plain interiors. Odd in scale, spatial illusion and gravity of mood, these images are fittingly painted in a solid, workmanlike style and are startlingly prescient of 19th-century French artists like Courbet and Manet. Collective anonymity has always seemed appropriate to the way the Le Nain brought painting down to earth, but this rare overview of 44 paintings — including less radical religious works — also presents new scholarship regarding which brother did what. (Kimbell Art Museum, Fort Worth, 817-332-8451; kimbellart.org)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE7/22GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 506, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE7/22GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 506}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE7/22GUIDE7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 864, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE7/22GUIDE7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1519, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE7/22GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE7/22GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/arts/22GUIDE7/22GUIDE7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Le Nain Brothers’ Prescient Work at Kimbell Art Museum', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; Prescient Works in Rare Overview', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 