 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Biography', '_id': 'nyt://article/d22999e4-74f6-571b-a0ba-3245298d5b9c', 'word_count': 1382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d22999e4-74f6-571b-a0ba-3245298d5b9c'}, {'abstract': 'Recent versions of Apple’s OS X and iOS operating system software offer an easy way to share music, videos and other items between family members.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/21/technology/personaltech/sharing-itunes-store-purchases.html', 'snippet': 'Recent versions of Apple’s OS X and iOS operating system software offer an easy way to share music, videos and other items between family members.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. My husband and I started using the same account and password for iTunes years ago, before iCloud, but now it’s a problem. Neither of us wants to lose the music or apps on our authorized shared devices, but it is a real hassle that my phone messages get sent to him, and a variety of other issues have cropped up from sharing this account and password. How can I get my own account and still share the music and apps that our household has paid for?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/technology/personaltech/21techtipwebART/21techtipwebART-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1065, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/21/technology/personaltech/21techtipwebART/21techtipwebART-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1065}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/technology/personaltech/21techtipwebART/21techtipwebART-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 577, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ