Aurelien Breeden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aurelien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Breeden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5429abb8-d783-558e-962b-97cc0231bda8', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5429abb8-d783-558e-962b-97cc0231bda8'}, {'abstract': 'Stunt, derived from cheerleading, is gaining popularity in New York City public schools.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/05/20/who-you-calling-cheerleader/', 'snippet': 'Stunt, derived from cheerleading, is gaining popularity in New York City public schools.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One recent Saturday on Staten Island, Brooke Winkler surveyed the pack of teenage girls before her with an expression of barely suppressed fury. Just 20 minutes before the start of its game against Morrisania Educational Campus, from the Bronx, the Susan E. Wagner High School stunt team was fumbling its way through practice. The girls’ landings teetered, their timing lagged and one flier couldn’t seem to maintain her balance. And then there was the matter of congeniality.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who You Calling Cheerleader?', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who You Calling Cheerleader?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheerleaders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interscholastic Athletics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-20T15:18:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noah Remnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Remnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81a89982-fe01-5cf8-bff8-7a3d0bd00625', 'word_count': 939, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81a89982-fe01-5cf8-bff8-7a3d0bd00625'}, {'abstract': 'Elegant pieces represent the Eternal City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/20/t-magazine/fashion/italian-jewelry-designers.html', 'snippet': 'Elegant pieces represent the Eternal City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Roman jeweler’s brooches, rings and earrings are inspired by the 17th-century Baroque aesthetic of Borromini and Bernini, and designed, he says, to coax their wearers into upright postures and engender “regal attitudes.” Lavish gemstone mosaics — lemon quartz, green tourmalines, rose rubies — are combined with delicate miniatures painted on copper.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/20/t-magazine/fashion/jewelry-slide-WSSE/jewelry-slide-WSSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/20/t-magazine/fashion/jewelry-slide-WSSE/jewelry-slide-WSSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/20/t-magazine/fashion/jewelry-slide-WSSE/jewelry-slide-WSSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/20/t-magazine/fashion/jewelry-slide-WSSE/jewelry-slide-WSSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1683, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit'