Face,” and lauding others, like Jim Jarmusch’s “Paterson.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANNES, France — A week into the Cannes Film Festival, the machine-gun-carrying soldiers vanished. For the first stretch of this event, a cluster of soldiers had been patrolling the main shopping drag that runs parallel to the promenade overlooking the Mediterranean, just one part of the show of force this year. France remains in a state of emergency, but festivalgoers exist in a bubble, and, in time, talk about heavy security gave way to other concerns, like whether Maren Ade’s “Toni Erdmann” would win the Palme d’Or, and if Sean Penn’s “The Last Face” was worse than Nicolas Winding Refn’s “The Neon Demon” or Xavier Dolan’s “It’s Only the End of the World.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21CANNESREPORT/21CANNESREPORT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21CANNESREPORT/21CANNESREPORT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21CANNESREPORT/21CANNESREPORT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21CANNESREPORT/21CANNESREPORT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1370, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21CANNESREPORT/21CANNESREPORT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21CANNESREPORT/21CANNESREPORT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21CANNESREPORT/21CANNESREPORT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cannes Keeps Its Traditions, Including Its Boos', 'kicker': "Critic's Notebook", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cannes Keeps Its Traditions, Like Boos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannes International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jarmusch, Jim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Paterson (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Penn, Sean', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Last Face (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Refn, Nicolas Winding', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Neon Demon (Movie)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leaud, Jean-Pierre (1944- )', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Serra, Albert', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Gimme Danger (Movie)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pop, Iggy', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-20T21:45:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1183a48-4d42-580a-83c2-5cd3bf1bce93', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1183a48-4d42-580a-83c2-5cd3bf1bce93'}, {'abstract': 'The crash of the EgyptAir flight on Thursday morning has raised fears among travelers. Here is what you need to know about safety in the skies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/20/travel/frequently-asked-questions-terrorism-on-planes.html', 'snippet': 'The crash of the EgyptAir flight on Thursday morning has raised fears among travelers. Here is what you need to know about safety in the skies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fact that Egyptian authorities said that they were looking into whether terrorism had caused the crash of the EgyptAir flight from Paris to Cairo early Thursday morning raised fears about the safety of air travel and also traveling in general. Here’s what you need to know about staying safe in the skies and on the ground.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Frequently Asked Questions: Air Travel and EgyptAir', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aviation Accidents, Safety and Disasters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airport Security', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-20T21:45:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99cd94f8-5b56-5992-895a-ae32552c15e7', 'word_count': 771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99cd94f8-5b56-5992-895a-ae32552c15e7'}, {'abstract': 'The final Artists Space exhibition at 38 Greene Street stars Lukas Duwenhögger, a mesmerizing German artist who is barely known in this country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/21/arts/design/your-last-chance-to-see-one-of-new-yorks-great-gallery-spaces.html', 'snippet': 'The final Artists Space exhibition at 38 Greene Street stars Lukas Duwenhögger, a mesmerizing German artist who is barely known in this country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 35-year survey of the work of Lukas Duwenhögger at Artists Space provides a full, mesmerizing view of a German artist, now 60 and based in Istanbul, who is barely known in this country. Mr. Duwenhögger is a sly, seductive stylist and storyteller, adept at figurative painting as well as film, sculpture and suavely domestic installations — all guided by a decorative sensibility that is both between-the-wars and among cultures. Alternately theatrical and intimate, with a palette of saturated pastels as complex as their narratives, his paintings especially depict a gay subculture at once conjured, allegorical and real. His larger subjects include human connection and vulnerability. The show’s subtitle, “Undoolay,” was invented by Edith Wharton in “The Custom of the Country” as “the French for crimping.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21ARTSPACE/21ARTSPACE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21ARTSPACE/21ARTSPACE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21ARTSPACE/21ARTSPACE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21ARTSPACE/21ARTSPACE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1369, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21ARTSPACE/21ARTSPACE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21ARTSPACE/21ARTSPACE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/21/arts/21ARTSPACE/21ARTSPACE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Last Chance to See One of New York’s Great Gallery Spaces', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Sly, Seductive Farewell to 38 Greene Street', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARTISTS SPACE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Lukas Duwenhogger: Undoolay (Exhibit)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duwenhogger, Lukas (1956- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-20T21:48:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0350bf4-3e42-5e91-af7a-2faf8035297d', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0350bf4-3e42-5e91-af7a-2faf8035297d'}, {'abstract': 'A surprise of the spring party season was the number of men who shed their black-tie uniform for dinner jackets that had flower patterns, were made of brocade or were bejeweled.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/22/fashion/bill-cunningham-peacocking.html', 'snippet': 'A surprise of the spring party season was the number of men who shed their black-tie uniform for dinner jackets that had flower patterns, were made of brocade or were bejeweled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A surprise of the spring party season was the number of men who shed their black-tie uniform for dinner jackets that had flower patterns, were made of brocade, were bejeweled or featured wild flights of fantasy on the lapels. The New York social calendar still offers very formal occasions, organized by the Metropolitan Opera and Met Museum, where men wear white tie and tails. Brocade fabrics came in shades of blue and velvet . In the late ’60s and ’70s, the peacock revolution spread to men’s dinner jackets, but they quickly reverted to traditional black tie.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/fashion/22STREETCOMBO2/22STREETCOMBO2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/fashion/22STREETCOMBO2/22STREETCOMBO2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/fashion/22STREETCOMBO2/22STREETCOMBO2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/fashion/22STREETCOMB