n the chairman of MetLife, which received considerable subsidies from the city on the condition that it keep rents low.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 605, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 605}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1016, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1983, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L/22ALBUMSS-slide-CP2L-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Stuyvesant Town, a Child’s Utopia', 'kicker': 'Album', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Child’s Utopia in Rabbit Town', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stuyvesant Town (Manhattan, NY, Apartments)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Peter Cooper Village (Manhattan, NY, Apartments)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Affordable Housing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-20T23:18:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/353b02ac-6d0e-553d-94a5-be60234e5de8', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/353b02ac-6d0e-553d-94a5-be60234e5de8'}, {'abstract': 'For the city equestrian, a rare breed in itself, the only remaining shop of its kind on Old Stable Row, otherwise known as East 24th Street, is Manhattan Saddlery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/22/nyregion/manhattan-saddlery-is-still-riding-tall-on-old-stable-row.html', 'snippet': 'For the city equestrian, a rare breed in itself, the only remaining shop of its kind on Old Stable Row, otherwise known as East 24th Street, is Manhattan Saddlery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When horseback riding was the dominant mode of transportation, equestrian shops were as common as fresh-pressed juice bars are today. East 24th Street was so populated with places to outfit and care for horses that it came to be known as Old Stable Row. Times have changed. And for the city equestrian, a rare breed in itself, the only remaining shop of its kind on Old Stable Row is Manhattan Saddlery.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22JOINT1/22JOINT1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22JOINT1/22JOINT1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22JOINT1/22JOINT1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22JOINT1/22JOINT1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22JOINT1/22JOINT1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22JOINT1/22JOINT1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22JOINT1/22JOINT1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Manhattan Saddlery Is Still Riding Tall on Old Stable Row', 'kicker': 'Neighborhood Joint', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Riding Tall on Old Stable Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flatiron District (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horsemanship and Equestrian Events', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Protective Clothing and Gear', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-20T23:27:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gloria Dawson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gloria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dawson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c0bd153-0d64-58ec-b012-58248d914dc8', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c0bd153-0d64-58ec-b012-58248d914dc8'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Straub, whose book “Modern Lovers” comes out this month, wakes up early with her husband and two young kids, watches cheesy television and always keeps a good mozzarella on hand for pizza.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/22/nyregion/how-emma-straub-novelist-spends-her-sundays.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Straub, whose book “Modern Lovers” comes out this month, wakes up early with her husband and two young kids, watches cheesy television and always keeps a good mozzarella on hand for pizza.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Emma Straub wrote her best-selling novel “The Vacationers” when she was pregnant with her first child, and her new novel, “Modern Lovers,” which comes out May 31, while pregnant with her second. “It’s all part of this giant cycle of book-baby-book-baby,” Ms. Straub, 36, said. The process seems to work: Vogue selected “Modern Lovers” as one of its top books this month, saying that it “nails the Brooklyn mise-en-scène: $2 million Victorians, a kombucha-brewing enterprise gone awry.” She lives with her husband, Michael Fusco-Straub, 37, a graphic designer, and their sons, River, 2, and Miles, 4 months, in a house in Brooklyn’s Columbia Street Waterfront District. Soon, the family will go on book tour — in outfits that match the pattern on the book cover. “I am hoping they will be so amused by the fact that I’m wearing my book jacket that they won’t notice the bags under my eyes,” Ms. Straub said.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 509, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 509}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 868, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1320, 'width': 1557, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Emma Straub, Novelist, Spends Her Sundays', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 