'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f8e7c0f-8f3a-59a8-a1b5-51ca93242b4b', 'word_count': 527, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f8e7c0f-8f3a-59a8-a1b5-51ca93242b4b'}, {'abstract': 'The confirmation of Eric Fanning as Army secretary provides a good opportunity to take stock of the nation’s progress.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/22/opinion/sunday/an-openly-gay-man-runs-the-army.html', 'snippet': 'The confirmation of Eric Fanning as Army secretary provides a good opportunity to take stock of the nation’s progress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week an openly gay man, Eric Fanning, became secretary of the Army. Read that sentence again and contemplate what it reveals about how much and how quickly American society has changed. Only five years ago, openly gay people were barred from serving in its armed forces. During Mr. Fanning’s lengthy confirmation process, his sexual orientation was simply not an issue. That is a tribute to those who fought so hard to repeal the ban, and a measure of the nation’s at times uncertain, but as yet unfailing, march toward equality.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/opinion/sunday/22sun1/22sun1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 631, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/22/opinion/sunday/22sun1/22sun1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 631}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/opinion/sunday/22sun1/22sun1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 974, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/22/opinion/sunday/22sun1/22sun1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1948, 'subType': 'superJ