set on the first hole tee on God’s celestial golf course. The first half of the ride is a gentle ascent along the benchmarks of human communication throughout the centuries, from cave drawings to the printing press. But the world has changed since 1982, the day Walt Disney’s “Future World” opened, and so the last stage has been updated to show the inside of a garage, where a shaggy, bearded man is crouched over a desk, building the first home computer. It’s a mystery whether the man is supposed to be Steve Wozniak or Steve Jobs, but the iconography is central to the legend of Silicon Valley: Innovators in a garage, with big ideas and minimal resources, poised to change the world.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/arts/22SILICON/22SILICON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/23/arts/22SILICON/22SILICON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/arts/22SILICON/22SILICON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/arts/22SILICON/22SILICON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/arts/22SILICON/22SILICON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/23/arts/22SILICON/22SILICON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption