en Island, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-23T02:43:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Correal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Correal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a21c4c0-483d-5450-a0c7-847f90313754', 'word_count': 347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a21c4c0-483d-5450-a0c7-847f90313754'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s episode feels like a slight comedown after last week’s sensational half-hour.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/22/arts/television/veep-season-5-episode-5-thanksgiving.html', 'snippet': 'This week’s episode feels like a slight comedown after last week’s sensational half-hour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s episode of “Veep” feels like a slight comedown in the wake of last week’s sensational “Mother.” Perhaps that was inevitable. After a half-hour of television that featured the death of a loved one, the disappointment of a recount lost and a performance for the HBO comedy ages from Julia Louis-Dreyfus, an episode that deals with salmonella poisoning and turkey pardons doesn’t pack quite the same punch. “Veep” is so terrific that we’ve come to expect gold medal-worthy triumph every week. But there’s no shame in earning a bronze. Hey, as we learned in episode two of this season, a bronze still means a trip to the White House, right?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/20/arts/22VEEP/22VEEP-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/20/arts/22VEEP/22VEEP-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'c