_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Liberated by Uber and Lyft', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Liberated by Uber and Lyft', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Uber Technologies Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lyft Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Austin (Tex)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-23T07:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ca72d17-0d01-597e-80b0-38ec55af0b3c', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ca72d17-0d01-597e-80b0-38ec55af0b3c'}, {'abstract': 'The Ambulatory Surgery Center Association says Ms. Rivers’s death was due to human error, not the fact that she was in an outpatient clinic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/23/opinion/joan-rivers-and-outpatient-surgery.html', 'snippet': 'The Ambulatory Surgery Center Association says Ms. Rivers’s death was due to human error, not the fact that she was in an outpatient clinic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Joan Rivers and Outpatient Surgery', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Joan Rivers’s Surgery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Malpractice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgery and Surgeons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rivers, Joan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-23T07:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6458850-0714-57cd-b3e1-47fe47f2128d', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6458850-0714-57cd-b3e1-47fe47f2128d'}, {'abstract': '“Don’t ask, don’t tell” might be the new code for savvy Wall Streeters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/23/opinion/how-to-get-away-with-insider-trading.html', 'snippet': '“Don’t ask, don’t tell” might be the new code for savvy Wall Streeters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ANOTHER lurid case of insider trading has been uncovered: The former chairman of Dean Foods has admitted giving insider information to a well-known professional sports bettor in Las Vegas because he was mired in gambling debts. The sports bettor made an estimated $43 million over five years from this information, according to federal prosecutors. A second beneficiary of stock tips was the professional golfer Phil Mickelson, who had gambling debts of his own.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23coffeeSub/23coffeeSub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23coffeeSub/23coffeeSub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23coffeeSub/23coffeeSub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23coffeeSub/23coffeeSub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1359, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23coffeeSub/23coffeeSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23coffeeSub/23coffeeSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23coffeeSub/23coffeeSub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Get Away With Insider Trading', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Getting Away With Insider Trading', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coffee, John C Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mickelson, Phil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bharara, Preet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-23T07:21:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By John C. Coffee Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Coffee', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a06d9d0-7d3b-5e57-ad45-2e0897dfcb5b', 'word_count': 858, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a06d9d0-7d3b-5e57-ad45-2e0897dfcb5b'}, {'abstract': 'Schools need to give potential students with criminal records a chance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/23/opinion/college-applications-that-cross-a-line.html', 'snippet': 'Schools need to give potential students with criminal records a chance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Obama administration is rightly urging colleges and universities to re-evaluate how they use criminal-record information in admissions decisions. By asking about criminal convictions on their applications, the schools discourage applicants who are capable of performing academically at college and who present no danger to campus safety. The remedy is to stop asking about these records or at least delay the question until the applicant has received a provisional offer of acceptance.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon2web/23mon2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon2web/23mon2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon2web/23mon2web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon2web/23mon2web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1348, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon2web/23mon2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon2web/23mon2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon2web/23mon2web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'College Applications That Cross a Line', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'College Applications That Cross a Line', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ex-Convicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-23T07:21:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/affee7d6-b0dc-5bfc-b63c-0cace3022774', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/affee7d6-b0dc-5bfc-b63c-0cace3022774'}, {'abstract': 'Not necessarily. Politics is about identity, not issues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/23/opinion/campaign-stops/do-sanders-supporters-favor-his-policies.html', 'snippet': 'Not necessarily. Politics is about identity, not issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bernie Sanders is widely credited with pulling Hillary Clinton and the Democratic Party to the left on major issues like health care, trade, financial regulation and the minimum wage. Now he says he will battle all the way to the convention on behalf of “people who are prepared to fight for real economic and social change.” But the premise animating that battle — that Mr. Sanders’s surprising success in the primary race is because of his liberal policy positions — may be familiar and comforting, but it is greatly exaggerated.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/20/opinion/20bartelsWeb/20bartelsWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/20/opinion/20bartelsWeb/20bartelsWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/20/opinion/20bartelsWeb/20bartelsWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/20/opinion/20bartelsWeb/20bartelsWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/20/opinion/20bartelsWeb/20bartelsWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/20/opinion/20bartelsWeb/20bartelsWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/20/opinion/20bartelsWeb/20bartelsWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Sanders Supporters Favor His Policies?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanders, Bernard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Liberalism (US Politics)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-23T07:21:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Campaign Stops', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher H. Achen and Larry M. Bartels', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Achen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/058a603c-b791-55dc-9dc7-5a6b8484bf9c', 'word_count': 1105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/058a603c-b791-55dc-9dc7-5a6b8484bf9c'}, {'abstract': 'Europe’s limits on the content of political expression may be leading to unintended consequences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/23/opinion/letter-from-austria-is-europes-tolerant-society-backfiring.html', 'snippet': 'Europe’s limits on the content of political expression may be leading to unintended consequences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VIENNA — Not long ago, riding the subway with my 8-year-old son to his school here, I noticed a headline in a newspaper left on a nearby seat: “Schoolteacher sues Muslim father for refusing to shake hands with her.” Compared with the alarming rise of the far-right Freedom Party here in Austria in recent months, this was minor news, but resonant. It seemed to point in some way to the clash-of-civilizations narrative feeding the xenophobic brand of politics asce