ization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5045743-229b-572c-90c6-e2e646df47f6', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5045743-229b-572c-90c6-e2e646df47f6'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the unmourned thousands buried on Hart Island are given back their identities and respect.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/24/opinion/new-york-stories-from-potters-field.html', 'snippet': 'Some of the unmourned thousands buried on Hart Island are given back their identities and respect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As mighty a city as New York claims to be, its power and pride seem nowhere in evidence on Hart Island, a desolate spot off the Bronx shore where the most pauperous and forgotten citizens are buried in tiers of coffins for their eternal rest in a potter’s field. Since the Civil War, the lives and misfortunes of those anonymously interred in bulldozed pits have gone untold and unmourned while the rest of the city teems with life around them.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon3hart/23mon3hart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon3hart/23mon3hart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon3hart/23mon3hart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/23/opinion/23mon3hart/23mon3hart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1369, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail',