 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frick Collection', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-24T21:41:44+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/4fb9e678-523a-535e-87c3-3f8ef65a172d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/4fb9e678-523a-535e-87c3-3f8ef65a172d'}, {'abstract': 'A new show at the Frick Collection pairs the sculptor Arlene Shechet’s irreverent works with the Meissen classics that inspired them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/24/t-magazine/art/meissen-porcelain-sculpture-arlene-shechet-frick.html', 'snippet': 'A new show at the Frick Collection pairs the sculptor Arlene Shechet’s irreverent works with the Meissen classics that inspired them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lavish show of Meissen porcelain opens today at the Frick Collection — but don’t expect formal arrangements of Chinese vases, bird-shaped cruets and Rococo figurines. In a first, the Upper East Side museum has invited a contemporary artist to curate and design an exhibition of centuries-old objects with her own Meissen-inspired works alongside — and stacked on, leaning against and placed in other heretical and seemingly precarious positions in relation to it. The New York sculptor Arlene Shechet selected 130 pieces from the famed Arnhold Collection, a promised gift to the Frick, which is devoting its Portico Gallery to “Porcelain, No Simple Matter,” a radical and rowdy rethinking of the precious. In the slide show above, Shechet shares the thought processes behind five of the matchups on view.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/24/t-magazine/Shechet-slide-333C/Shechet-slide-333C-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 757, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'a