ght': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/business/26ARTIFICIAL/26ARTIFICIAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/26/business/26ARTIFICIAL/26ARTIFICIAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/business/26ARTIFICIAL/26ARTIFICIAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Artificial Intelligence Is Far From Matching Humans, Panel Says', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Close to Human, Artificial Intelligence Still Raises Concerns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Artificial Intelligence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Markoff, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of Science and Technology Policy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Washington', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seattle (Wash)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-25T18:59:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Markoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Markoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88c9e4f3-e3b4-5f9e-a369-a07fad8d21b7', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88c9e4f3-e3b4-5f9e-a369-a07fad8d21b7'}, {'abstract': 'Before finding music magic in North Carolina, Max Jury worked with truck drivers and recorded at Jimi Hendrix’s old studio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/25/t-magazine/entertainment/max-jury-gospel-singer-songwriter.html', 'snippet': 'Before finding music magic in North Carolina, Max Jury worked with truck drivers and recorded at Jimi Hendrix’s old studio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Max Jury hadn’t planned it this way. The 24-year-old singer-songwriter and Iowa native figured he’d attend the prestigious Berklee College of Music, cobble together enough songs for an album and be on his way to music stardom. “I was a bit naïve,” admits Jury, whose self-titled debut album is out June 3. He did attend Berklee, but after feeling stifled by the clinical nature of his classes, dropped out after just a year, returning instead to his hometown, Des Moines, and taking a job as a maintenance worker for the local parks department — an experience that left him dispirited. “Me and manual labor don’t really mix,” Jury says, adding that his co-workers — mostly truck drivers picking up work in their off-season — “didn’t treat me very nice.” This newfound sense of dislocation, coupled with his then-girlfriend’s moving to New York, inspired Jury to write the album track “Numb,” a video for which premieres here. “I was just kind of directionless and thinking, ‘Well, shoot, what am I doing?’”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/25/t-magazine/maxjury/maxjury-articleLarge.png', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/25/t-magazine/maxjury/maxjury-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/25/t-magazine/maxjury/maxjury-jumbo.png', 'height': 554, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/25/t-magazine/maxjury/maxjury-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1108, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/25/t-magazine/maxjury/maxjury-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/25/t-magazine/maxjury/maxjury-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/25/t-magazine/maxjury/maxjury-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How a Former Parks & Rec Employee Found His Gospel Voice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-25T19:01:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Entertainment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Oloizia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oloizia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e71125e0-32d9-528a-89f7-a5d14ed821e2', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e71125e0-32d9-528a-89f7-a5d14ed821e2'}, {'abstr