, {'abstract': 'The show, at the Lyric Theater, features a story, set in old Hollywood, that adds the company’s usual circus acts to a traditional musical.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/26/theater/review-paramour-brings-cirque-du-soleil-to-broadway.html', 'snippet': 'The show, at the Lyric Theater, features a story, set in old Hollywood, that adds the company’s usual circus acts to a traditional musical.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pity the poor chanteuse. Flame-haired, beautiful and buxom, clad in a spangly dress and draped seductively against a piano, she’s singing her heart out, pouring her soul into a song about … er, something. Love? Loss? Her favorite nail salon?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Paramour’ Brings Cirque du Soleil to Broadway', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Actors  Onstage, and Often Above It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cirque du Soleil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Decoufle, Philippe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlsson, Andreas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dubuc, Guy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lessard, Marc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Paramour (Play)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hyler, West (1977- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-26T02:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/577c140c-568a-5be2-aad1-cf4f18cc4a1b', 'word_count': 964, 'uri': 'nyt://article/577c140c-568a-5be2-aad1-cf4f18cc4a1b'}, {'abstract': 'Theater for a New Audience has paired “A Doll’s House” and “The Father,” with Maggie Lacey and John Douglas Thompson playing the leads in both, at the Polonsky Shakespeare Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/26/theater/review-domestic-doom-fiercely-conveyed-in-ibsen-and-strindberg-plays.html', 'snippet': 'Theater for a New Audience has paired “A Doll’s House” and “The Father,” with Maggie Lacey and John Douglas Thompson playing the leads in both, at the Polonsky Shakespeare Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A noise of primal desperation emanates from each of the two suspenseful dramas that have been resonantly paired in repertory by Theater for a New Audience. They are Ibsen’s “A Doll’s House” and Strindberg’s “The Father.” And there comes a moment about halfway through both productions when a solitary soul is heard gasping as if her last breath were at hand.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26DOLLFATHER1/26DOLLFATHER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26DOLLFATHER1/26DOLLFATHER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26DOLLFATHER1/26DOLLFATHER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26DOLLFATHER1/26DOLLFATHER1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26DOLLFATHER1/26DOLLFATHER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26DOLLFATHER1/26DOLLFATHER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26DOLLFATHER1/26DOLLFATHER1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Spouses Gasping for Air in Twinned Ibsen and Strindberg Plays', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spouses Gasping for Air on Parallel Battlefields', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ibsen, Henrik', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strindberg, August', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Theater for a New Audience', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Father (Play)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arbus, Arin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "A Doll's House (Play)", 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-26T02:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb9f0e44-4830-521f-9d5f-1e607674b5f7', 'word_count': 1145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb9f0e44-4830-521f-9d5f-1e607674b5f7'}, {'abstract': 'John Doyle has done his imaginative best to tame, through shrinkage, one of the great, shape-shifting monsters of world theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/26/theater/review-a-minimalist-version-of-peer-gynt-still-provokes.html', 'snippet': 'John Doyle has done his imaginative best to tame, through shrinkage, one of the great, shape-shifting monsters of world theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toward the end of Ibsen’s “Peer Gynt,” a saga of self under siege, the title character is discovered peeling an onion, finding in the layers of that humble vegetable a symbol for the chapters of an eventful life. In most productions of this bulky fantasia, the moment is a long time coming — as much as five or six hours — and audiences are likely to feel as depleted as the now aged Peer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26PEERGYNT/26PEERGYNT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26PEERGYNT/26PEERGYNT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26PEERGYNT/26PEERGYNT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26PEERGYNT/26PEERGYNT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/26/arts/26PEERGYNT/26PEERGYNT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'th