:01:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9adc3e7-c518-5517-9332-a69a1682cf9b', 'word_count': 524, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9adc3e7-c518-5517-9332-a69a1682cf9b'}, {'abstract': 'Eccentric, inventive and offbeat are words that describe the paintings, sculpture and photography of Mr. Samaras, but his pastels stand apart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/27/arts/design/the-morgan-bejeweled-with-the-pastels-of-lucas-samaras.html', 'snippet': 'Eccentric, inventive and offbeat are words that describe the paintings, sculpture and photography of Mr. Samaras, but his pastels stand apart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in the late 1950s, when ambitious painters were obliged to produce big, bold abstractions, Lucas Samaras took up pastel, a fragile, intimate medium that had not given rise to history-making oeuvres since the days of Edgar Degas and Odilon Redon. The maverick Mr. Samaras, now 79, went on to forge a singular, nearly 60-year career of eccentric invention and offbeat imagination in painting, sculpture and photography. But he periodically returned to pastel to create small, vibrantly colorful and poetically captivating images.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/27/arts/27SAMARAS3/27SAMARAS3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 910, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/27/arts/27SAMARAS3/27SAMARAS3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 910}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non