l': 'By Jarle Breivik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jarle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Breivik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e90e156-ea86-5d54-b4b0-8e19f1c2d6f9', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e90e156-ea86-5d54-b4b0-8e19f1c2d6f9'}, {'abstract': 'In handling the case of a police officer who killed a driver, the district attorney defied an order from Gov. Andrew Cuomo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/27/opinion/move-quickly-against-a-rogue-da.html', 'snippet': 'In handling the case of a police officer who killed a driver, the district attorney defied an order from Gov. Andrew Cuomo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo took a necessary step last year when he empowered New York’s attorney general to take control of cases in which police officers may have unjustifiably killed unarmed citizens. His order, enacted several months after a New York grand jury failed to indict the officer who killed Eric Garner, was intended to assure the public that police officers who kill civilians would be prosecuted fairly and without bias.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/27/opinion/27fri3web/27fri3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 447, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/27/opinion/27fri3web/27fri3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 447}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/27/opinion/27fri3web/27fri3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/0