r': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-27T07:21:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Héctor Tobar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Héctor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tobar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a78f1786-a759-5036-bc7a-e2b0fbfec4ad', 'word_count': 953, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a78f1786-a759-5036-bc7a-e2b0fbfec4ad'}, {'abstract': 'The idea of selling shares in the kingdom’s oil company, Aramco, cannot be viewed as a major step in modernizing the nation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/27/opinion/a-saudi-ipo-buyer-beware.html', 'snippet': 'The idea of selling shares in the kingdom’s oil company, Aramco, cannot be viewed as a major step in modernizing the nation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January, when the Saudi royal family first floated the idea of selling shares in Saudi Aramco, the kingdom’s oil company, many analysts regarded the proposal as an intriguing nonstarter. They were intrigued because even a partial privatization could create a company larger than Exxon Mobil, the world’s largest publicly owned oil company. They were skeptical because initial public offerings on global exchanges require disclosure and scrutiny, as well as a record of transparent corporate governance. These are not Aramco’s strong points.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/27/opinion/27fri2web/27fri2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/27/opinion/27fri2web/27fri2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 's