organizations', 'value': 'Space Exploration Technologies Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-27T23:43:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/650535e6-86b1-5189-8924-a9a4994266f4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/650535e6-86b1-5189-8924-a9a4994266f4'}, {'abstract': '“The Curious Case of Kiryas Joel” retraces the political maneuvering that allowed a religious group in Orange County to establish its own school system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/29/nyregion/the-hasidic-school-district-that-was-created-in-secret.html', 'snippet': '“The Curious Case of Kiryas Joel” retraces the political maneuvering that allowed a religious group in Orange County to establish its own school system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even most New Yorkers have forgotten that Congress passed the Bill of Rights in New York. According to “The Curious Case of Kiryas Joel: The Rise of a Village Theocracy and the Battle to Defend the Separation of Church and State” (Chicago Review Press, $27.99), New York State is also where the “establishment clause” of the First Amendment’s freedom of religion protections was cynically abridged exactly two centuries later.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 538, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1076, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/05/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hasidic School District That Was Created in Secret', 'kicker': 'Bookshelf', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hasidim and the Cynical Politicians', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grumet, Louis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Caher, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hackett, Kathleen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kiryas Joel (NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Curious Case of Kiryas Joel: The Rise of a Village Theocracy and the Battle to Defend the Separation of Church and State (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Brooklyn Interiors (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-27T23:43:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11414145-1863-550b-bd14-20be9fd8a984', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11414145-1863-550b-bd14-20be9fd8a984'}, {'abstract': 'In late spring, the crabs return to shallow bays in and around New York to mate after a winter in deeper water. Birds that feast on their eggs wait hungrily.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/29/nyregion/time-for-horseshoe-crabs-and-the-shorebirds-that-love-them.html', 'snippet': 'In late spring, the crabs return to shallow bays in and around New York to mate after a winter in deeper water. Birds that feast on their eggs wait hungrily.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Humans may be quick to judge, but only one horseshoe crab can ultimately gauge the loveliness of another. When these crabs appear on a beach — suddenly and in large numbers — they can seem fearsome and even malevolent, and that’s before you glimpse beneath their intimidating, pointy-tailed carapace at a mouth set in the center of 12 madly churning legs. Beauty, in this case, is in the compound eyes of the beholder: Horseshoe crabs each have a total of 10 eyes, through which they obviously find one another appealing. These trilobite relatives have been attracting mates successfully for well over 400 million years.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/29/nyregion/29NATURE/29NATURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/29/nyregion/29NATURE/29NATURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'cred