on Sunday afternoon was patently lopsided. The first half consisted of the world premiere of a 10-minute work specially commissioned from an emerging composer. After the intermission came Mahler’s “Resurrection” Symphony, an hour-and-a-half-long meditation on life, death and redemption for a mammoth orchestra with an additional offstage brass section, two vocal soloists and a large choir.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: The New York Youth Symphony, Empowered at Carnegie Hall', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Empowered by the Tricky and Irregular', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'da Fonseca-Wollheim, Corinna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Faegre, Brendan (1985- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mahler, Gustav', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Youth Symphony', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Collective (Musical Work)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Resurrection Symphony (Musical Work)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-30T21:07:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f105bbaf-2a55-545e-a9cb-8c931095a4c0', 'word_count': 466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f105bbaf-2a55-545e-a9cb-8c931095a4c0'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Straub’s new novel describes the love and second thoughts of two families in the transitioning neighborhood of Ditmas Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/05/31/books/review-modern-lovers-by-emma-straub.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Straub’s new novel describes the love and second thoughts of two families in the transitioning neighborhood of Ditmas Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an epilogue to Emma Straub’s charming new book, we learn that the story’s teenage hero, Harry Marx, ends up going to Brown University, where he proposes to submit a novel as his honors thesis: “The novel will be inspired by the tropes of classic love stories such as ‘Romeo and Juliet’ and ‘Tristan and Isolde,’ set in modern-day Ditmas Park, Brooklyn, with two neighboring families falling in and out of love simultaneously.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/31/arts/31BOOKSTRAUB1/31BOOKSTRAUB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/05/31/arts/31BOOKSTRAUB1/31BOOKSTRAUB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 871}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/31/arts/31BOOKSTRAUB1/31BOOKSTRAUB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 706, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/31/arts/31BOOKSTRAUB1/31BOOKSTRAUB1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1412, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/05/31/arts/31BOOKSTRAUB1/31BOOKSTRAUB1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 