e-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 1050, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/opinion/01chappatte/01chappatte-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/06/01/opinion/01chappatte/01chappatte-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chappatte on How to Survive Airport Lines', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airport Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Security Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-31T16:36:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Chappatte', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chappatte', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa42901e-115a-5a27-8b94-61c18e54b6dc', 'word_count': 5, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa42901e-115a-5a27-8b94-61c18e54b6dc'}, {'abstract': 'A seasonal Japanese tea arrives in New York for a limited engagement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/06/01/dining/sencha-green-tea-shincha.html', 'snippet': 'A seasonal Japanese tea arrives in New York for a limited engagement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s more to Japanese green tea than the hyper-trendy powdered matcha. Aficionados eagerly await the year’s first leaves of sencha tea. This seasonal early-blooming tea is called shincha and will be available until the end of June. The deep-green leaf slivers look like grass cuttings and yield a golden-colored tea of delicate herbal elegance with an alluring sweetness in the aftertaste: Ippodo Shincha, $9.98 to $54.44, depending on the size, 125 East 39th Street, ippodo-tea.co.jp/en/.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-TEA/01BURNER-TEA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-TEA/01BURNER-TEA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 433}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-TEA/01BURNER-TEA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 739, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-TEA/01BURNER-TEA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1308, 'width': 1812, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-TEA/01BURNER-TEA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-TEA/01BURNER-TEA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-TEA/01BURNER-TEA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Leaf for the Tea Obsessive', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Sip: Matcha Isn’t the Only Japanese Green Tea', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-05-31T16:36:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00aff07c-a0d3-504b-a761-5538590ed75e', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00aff07c-a0d3-504b-a761-5538590ed75e'}, {'abstract': 'Vin des Amis sells natural, biodynamic and organic wines on a gritty edge of SoHo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/06/01/dining/wine-vin-des-amis-soho.html', 'snippet': 'Vin des Amis sells natural, biodynamic and organic wines on a gritty edge of SoHo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although it’s on a major thoroughfare, this very particular wine shop is out of the way, on the grittiest edge of SoHo. “There’s nothing like it in this area,” said Vinicus Aboin, the manager, who is originally from Brazil and hit it off with the owner, Ivanete Oliveira, a former importer who is also Brazilian. The shop’s emphasis is on natural, biodynamic and organic wines, and on labels you don’t see everywhere. “We are interested in the winemakers,” Mr. Aboin said, adding that these wines sometimes need explaining. But, he said, “When customers try the wines, they usually come back and buy more.” There are quite a few wines in magnums, including German rieslings in handsome tall bottles: Vin des Amis Wine,375-B Canal Street (West Broadway), 646-918-6141, vindesamis.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-WINE/01BURNER-WINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-WINE/01BURNER-WINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-WINE/01BURNER-WINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-WINE/01BURNER-WINE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-WINE/01BURNER-WINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-WINE/01BURNER-WINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/06/01/dining/01BURNER-WINE/01BURNER-WINE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Let the Wine Seller Be Your Guide', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Shop: A New Wine Frontier Opens on Canal Street', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vin des Amis Wine (Manhattan, NY, Retailer)', 'rank': 3, 'maj